-- Original English and Logical Reasoning MCQs, written for Examwise against the PMDC MDCAT 2025 curriculum (English
-- learning outcomes 1.1 to 3.4, Logical Reasoning themes 5.1 to 5.6) and the NUMS MDCAT 2026 English benchmarks.
-- English ENG-0010 to ENG-0295 (286 questions), Logical Reasoning LR-0010 to LR-0136 (127 questions).
-- Not taken from any book: commercial prep books were used only to see which topics and question styles are common.
-- Each question's source names the curriculum learning outcome it tests. Not in the free set.
--
-- ENG-0001..0009 and LR-0001..0009 are the UHS 2026 past paper (migration 0020), so these start at 0010.
-- English now has one MDCAT chapter per curriculum topic instead of two broad ones; the end of this file moves the
-- past-paper English questions into the new chapters (it changes nothing if they are not there).
--
-- Banks: English goes in MDCAT (its own chapter), AKU, and NUMS. NUMS leaves out the purely literary items (figures of
-- speech, irony, satire, tone), which its English benchmarks do not cover. Logical Reasoning is only on the national
-- MDCAT (NUMS has no LR section; the bank list has none for AKU), so it gets no bank_questions rows.
-- INSERT OR IGNORE keeps the file safe to run twice; if the site owner's admin account doesn't exist (a fresh local
-- database) nothing is inserted. Inserted in batches of 20 to stay under D1's statement size limit.

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions
  (id, subject, chapter, topic, difficulty, source, stem, options, answer, why, wrong, stage, free, checks, writer_id, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT s.column1, s.column2, s.column3, s.column4, s.column5, s.column6, s.column7, s.column8, s.column9, s.column10, s.column11,
  'live', 0, '[]', u.id, unixepoch() * 1000, unixepoch() * 1000
FROM (VALUES
  ('ENG-0010', 'English', 'Vocabulary in context', 'Meaning from context', 'easy', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.1', 'In the sentence below, the word "deft" most nearly means:
"Even after a twelve-hour operation, the surgeon''s movements remained deft and precise."', '["skilful and quick", "clumsy", "slow", "hesitant"]', 0, 'Deft means neat, skilful and quick in movement; ''precise'' in the same sentence supports this.', '{"1": "Clumsy is the opposite of deft.", "2": "Deft movements are quick, not slow.", "3": "Hesitant means unsure, which does not fit ''precise''."}'),
  ('ENG-0011', 'English', 'Vocabulary in context', 'Meaning from context', 'moderate', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.1', 'In the sentence below, the word "scathing" most nearly means:
"Despite the critics'' scathing review, the film drew large crowds."', '["highly favourable", "severely critical", "very lengthy", "hard to understand"]', 1, 'Scathing means harshly critical. ''Despite'' signals a contrast: the film did well even though the review attacked it.', '{"0": "A favourable review would not set up the contrast that ''despite'' signals.", "2": "Scathing describes the tone of a review, not its length.", "3": "Scathing says nothing about how easy the review was to understand."}'),
  ('ENG-0012', 'English', 'Vocabulary in context', 'Meaning from context', 'easy', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.1', 'In the sentence below, the word "verbose" most nearly means:
"The speech was so verbose that many listeners lost interest before he reached his main point."', '["too quiet to hear", "deliberately offensive", "using more words than needed", "short and to the point"]', 2, 'Verbose means wordy. Listeners lost interest before the main point because the speaker used far more words than necessary.', '{"0": "Verbose is about the number of words, not volume.", "1": "Verbose does not mean offensive.", "3": "This is the opposite of verbose (concise)."}'),
  ('ENG-0013', 'English', 'Vocabulary in context', 'Meaning from context', 'easy', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.1', 'In the sentence below, the word "alleviated" most nearly means:
"The new painkiller alleviated the patient''s discomfort within minutes."', '["worsened", "measured", "caused", "relieved"]', 3, 'To alleviate is to make pain or suffering less severe, i.e. to relieve it.', '{"0": "Worsened is the opposite meaning.", "1": "A painkiller does not measure pain.", "2": "A painkiller does not cause discomfort."}'),
  ('ENG-0014', 'English', 'Vocabulary in context', 'Meaning from context', 'moderate', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.1', 'In the sentence below, the word "candid" most nearly means:
"Her candid reply surprised the committee, who had expected a rehearsed and evasive answer."', '["frank and honest", "polite but vague", "nervous", "carefully prepared"]', 0, 'Candid means frank and truthful. It contrasts with the ''rehearsed and evasive'' answer the committee expected.', '{"1": "Vague is close to ''evasive'', which is what the reply was NOT.", "2": "Candid describes honesty, not nervousness.", "3": "Carefully prepared matches ''rehearsed'', the opposite of what she gave."}'),
  ('ENG-0015', 'English', 'Vocabulary in context', 'Meaning from context', 'easy', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.1', 'In the sentence below, the word "meagre" most nearly means:
"The village''s water supply was so meagre that families had to ration every bucket."', '["polluted", "scanty; not enough", "plentiful", "far away"]', 1, 'Meagre means small in quantity and insufficient, which is why families had to ration water.', '{"0": "Polluted water is a quality problem; the sentence is about quantity.", "2": "Plentiful is the opposite of meagre.", "3": "Distance is not the meaning of meagre."}'),
  ('ENG-0016', 'English', 'Vocabulary in context', 'Meaning from context', 'easy', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.1', 'In the sentence below, the word "meticulous" most nearly means:
"She was known for her meticulous notes, in which not a single detail was left out."', '["careless", "colourful", "extremely careful and thorough", "brief"]', 2, 'Meticulous means showing great attention to detail; ''not a single detail was left out'' confirms it.', '{"0": "Careless is the opposite.", "1": "Meticulous is about care, not colour.", "3": "Notes with every detail are not brief."}'),
  ('ENG-0017', 'English', 'Vocabulary in context', 'Meaning from context', 'easy', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.1', 'In the sentence below, the word "impair" most nearly means:
"Lack of sleep can impair a student''s ability to concentrate."', '["improve", "measure", "reveal", "weaken"]', 3, 'To impair is to damage or weaken something, such as a function or ability.', '{"0": "Improve is the opposite.", "1": "Impair does not mean measure.", "2": "Impair does not mean reveal."}'),
  ('ENG-0018', 'English', 'Vocabulary in context', 'Meaning from context', 'moderate', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.1', 'In the sentence below, the word "precarious" most nearly means:
"The old building was in such a precarious state that engineers ordered everyone out."', '["dangerously unstable", "completely secure", "historically important", "overcrowded"]', 0, 'Precarious means insecure and likely to collapse or fail, which is why the building was evacuated.', '{"1": "Secure is the opposite of precarious.", "2": "Historical value would not be a reason to order everyone out.", "3": "Precarious describes instability, not crowding."}'),
  ('ENG-0019', 'English', 'Vocabulary in context', 'Meaning from context', 'moderate', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.1', 'In the sentence below, the word "reticent" most nearly means:
"Mehwish was reticent about her achievements and rarely mentioned them, even to friends."', '["boastful", "reserved; unwilling to speak", "unaware", "proud"]', 1, 'Reticent means not revealing one''s thoughts or feelings readily; she ''rarely mentioned'' her achievements.', '{"0": "Boastful is the opposite.", "2": "She knew about her achievements; she just did not talk about them.", "3": "Pride may lead to talking about achievements, the opposite of what she did."}'),
  ('ENG-0020', 'English', 'Vocabulary in context', 'Meaning from context', 'easy', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.1', 'In the sentence below, the word "boon" most nearly means:
"After months of drought, the heavy rain was a boon for farmers."', '["a disaster", "a warning", "a blessing", "a mystery"]', 2, 'A boon is something helpful or beneficial. Rain after a drought helps farmers.', '{"0": "After a drought, rain helps rather than harms farmers.", "1": "Boon does not mean warning.", "3": "Boon does not mean mystery."}'),
  ('ENG-0021', 'English', 'Vocabulary in context', 'Meaning from context', 'hard', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.1', 'In the sentence below, the word "obfuscate" most nearly means:
"The lawyer tried to obfuscate the issue with technical jargon so that the jury would lose track of the facts."', '["clarify", "summarise", "exaggerate", "make unclear"]', 3, 'To obfuscate is to make something obscure or confusing; the aim was for the jury to ''lose track of the facts''.', '{"0": "Clarify is the opposite.", "1": "A summary makes things clearer, not more confusing.", "2": "Exaggerating overstates; it is not the same as making unclear."}'),
  ('ENG-0022', 'English', 'Vocabulary in context', 'Meaning from context', 'moderate', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.1', 'In the sentence below, the word "abated" most nearly means:
"The fever abated by evening, and the child was able to eat again."', '["lessened", "rose sharply", "returned", "spread"]', 0, 'Abate means to become less intense. The child could eat again because the fever went down.', '{"1": "A rising fever would not let the child eat again.", "2": "Abated does not mean returned.", "3": "A fever does not ''spread'' in this sense."}'),
  ('ENG-0023', 'English', 'Vocabulary in context', 'Meaning from context', 'easy', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.1', 'In the sentence below, the word "frugal" most nearly means:
"His frugal habits allowed him to save enough money for university."', '["wasteful", "economical; careful with money", "unusual", "lazy"]', 1, 'Frugal means sparing or economical with money, which explains how he saved.', '{"0": "Wasteful habits would not lead to savings.", "2": "Frugal does not mean unusual.", "3": "Frugal has nothing to do with laziness."}'),
  ('ENG-0024', 'English', 'Vocabulary in context', 'Meaning from context', 'hard', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.1', 'In the sentence below, the word "cogent" most nearly means:
"The evidence was so cogent that even the sceptics on the panel were convinced."', '["weak", "lengthy", "convincing", "recent"]', 2, 'Cogent means clear, logical and convincing; it won over even the sceptics.', '{"0": "Weak evidence would not convince sceptics.", "1": "Cogent is about force of argument, not length.", "3": "Cogent is not about time."}'),
  ('ENG-0025', 'English', 'Vocabulary in context', 'Meaning from context', 'moderate', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.1', 'In the sentence below, the word "placate" most nearly means:
"Ali tried to placate his angry neighbour by offering to pay for the broken window."', '["provoke", "avoid", "blame", "calm down"]', 3, 'To placate is to make someone less angry; offering to pay was meant to calm the neighbour.', '{"0": "Provoke means to make angrier, the opposite.", "1": "Offering to pay is engaging with him, not avoiding him.", "2": "Offering to pay is not blaming him."}'),
  ('ENG-0026', 'English', 'Vocabulary in context', 'Meaning from context', 'hard', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.1', 'In the sentence below, the word "endemic" most nearly means:
"Malaria remains endemic in some low-lying districts, where cases occur every year."', '["regularly found in a particular area", "completely wiped out", "brought in only by travellers", "spreading suddenly across the world"]', 0, 'An endemic disease is constantly present in a particular region or population.', '{"1": "Wiped out (eradicated) is the opposite.", "2": "Cases occurring every year among locals is not only imported cases.", "3": "Sudden worldwide spread describes a pandemic."}'),
  ('ENG-0027', 'English', 'Vocabulary in context', 'Meaning from context', 'moderate', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.1', 'In the sentence below, the word "arbitrary" most nearly means:
"The decision seemed arbitrary: no reasons were given and no rules were followed."', '["fair and balanced", "based on random choice rather than reason", "final", "agreed by everyone"]', 1, 'Arbitrary means based on chance or personal whim rather than on reason or a system.', '{"0": "A decision with no reasons or rules is not shown to be fair.", "2": "Arbitrary does not mean final.", "3": "Arbitrary does not mean unanimous."}'),
  ('ENG-0028', 'English', 'Vocabulary in context', 'Meaning from context', 'easy', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.1', 'In the sentence below, the word "ubiquitous" most nearly means:
"Mobile phones have become ubiquitous; you see them in every home, shop and classroom."', '["very expensive", "extremely rare", "found everywhere", "harmful"]', 2, 'Ubiquitous means present everywhere, as the rest of the sentence shows.', '{"0": "Price is not the meaning of ubiquitous.", "1": "Rare is the opposite.", "3": "Ubiquitous says nothing about harm."}'),
  ('ENG-0029', 'English', 'Vocabulary in context', 'Meaning from context', 'easy', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.1', 'In the sentence below, the word "lucid" most nearly means:
"Even a beginner could follow the professor''s lucid explanation of the Krebs cycle."', '["long and tiring", "confusing", "humorous", "clear and easy to understand"]', 3, 'Lucid means expressed clearly; that is why even a beginner could follow it.', '{"0": "Lucid is not about length.", "1": "Confusing is the opposite.", "2": "Lucid does not mean funny."}')
) AS s
JOIN users u ON u.email = 'hozaiphaa@gmail.com';

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions
  (id, subject, chapter, topic, difficulty, source, stem, options, answer, why, wrong, stage, free, checks, writer_id, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT s.column1, s.column2, s.column3, s.column4, s.column5, s.column6, s.column7, s.column8, s.column9, s.column10, s.column11,
  'live', 0, '[]', u.id, unixepoch() * 1000, unixepoch() * 1000
FROM (VALUES
  ('ENG-0030', 'English', 'Vocabulary in context', 'Meaning from context', 'easy', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.1', 'In the sentence below, the word "prudent" most nearly means:
"It would be prudent to keep a copy of your admit card in case the original is lost."', '["wise and sensible", "dishonest", "unnecessary", "risky"]', 0, 'Prudent means acting with care and thought for the future, like keeping a spare copy.', '{"1": "Keeping a copy is not dishonest.", "2": "The sentence presents the copy as useful, not unnecessary.", "3": "Risky is the opposite of prudent."}'),
  ('ENG-0031', 'English', 'Vocabulary in context', 'Meaning from context', 'easy', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.1', 'In the sentence below, the word "diligent" most nearly means:
"Only the most diligent students complete all the practice papers before the exam."', '["gifted", "hard-working and careful", "careless", "lucky"]', 1, 'Diligent means showing steady effort and care in one''s work.', '{"0": "Gifted means naturally talented, not hard-working.", "2": "Careless is the opposite.", "3": "Luck has nothing to do with diligence."}'),
  ('ENG-0032', 'English', 'Synonyms and antonyms', 'Synonyms', 'easy', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.2', 'Choose the word closest in meaning to ABUNDANT.', '["scarce", "average", "plentiful", "valuable"]', 2, 'Abundant means existing in large quantities, i.e. plentiful.', '{"0": "Scarce is the opposite.", "1": "Average means ordinary, not plentiful.", "3": "Valuable refers to worth, not quantity."}'),
  ('ENG-0033', 'English', 'Synonyms and antonyms', 'Synonyms', 'easy', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.2', 'Choose the word closest in meaning to OBSOLETE.', '["modern", "broken", "costly", "outdated"]', 3, 'Obsolete means no longer in use because something newer has replaced it.', '{"0": "Modern is the opposite.", "1": "Something obsolete may still work; broken is a different idea.", "2": "Cost is unrelated."}'),
  ('ENG-0034', 'English', 'Synonyms and antonyms', 'Synonyms', 'moderate', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.2', 'Choose the word closest in meaning to LETHARGIC.', '["sluggish", "energetic", "anxious", "sleepless"]', 0, 'Lethargic means lacking energy, i.e. sluggish and drowsy.', '{"1": "Energetic is the opposite.", "2": "Anxious means worried.", "3": "Sleepless means unable to sleep, not lacking energy."}'),
  ('ENG-0035', 'English', 'Synonyms and antonyms', 'Synonyms', 'moderate', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.2', 'Choose the word closest in meaning to INNATE.', '["learned", "inborn", "hidden", "sudden"]', 1, 'Innate qualities are present from birth.', '{"0": "Learned (acquired) is the opposite.", "2": "Innate does not mean hidden.", "3": "Innate is not about suddenness."}'),
  ('ENG-0036', 'English', 'Synonyms and antonyms', 'Synonyms', 'moderate', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.2', 'Choose the word closest in meaning to CONCUR.', '["compete", "disagree", "agree", "conclude"]', 2, 'To concur is to agree with someone or something.', '{"0": "Compete is unrelated; it looks similar to ''concur'' only in spelling.", "1": "Disagree is the opposite.", "3": "Conclude means to finish or decide, not to agree."}'),
  ('ENG-0037', 'English', 'Synonyms and antonyms', 'Synonyms', 'moderate', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.2', 'Choose the word closest in meaning to AMBIGUOUS.', '["completely certain", "very ambitious", "openly hostile", "open to more than one meaning"]', 3, 'An ambiguous statement can be understood in more than one way.', '{"0": "Certain is the opposite of ambiguous.", "1": "Ambitious is a different word that only looks similar.", "2": "Hostile means unfriendly."}'),
  ('ENG-0038', 'English', 'Synonyms and antonyms', 'Synonyms', 'moderate', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.2', 'Choose the word closest in meaning to METICULOUS.', '["painstaking", "careless", "generous", "nervous"]', 0, 'Meticulous and painstaking both mean taking great care over details.', '{"1": "Careless is the opposite.", "2": "Generous means giving freely.", "3": "Nervous means anxious."}'),
  ('ENG-0039', 'English', 'Synonyms and antonyms', 'Antonyms', 'easy', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.2', 'Choose the word most nearly OPPOSITE in meaning to HOSTILE.', '["aggressive", "friendly", "unfriendly", "indifferent"]', 1, 'Hostile means unfriendly or aggressive, so friendly is its opposite.', '{"0": "Aggressive is close in meaning to hostile.", "2": "Unfriendly is a synonym, not an antonym.", "3": "Indifferent means not caring; it is neither friendly nor the true opposite."}'),
  ('ENG-0040', 'English', 'Synonyms and antonyms', 'Antonyms', 'moderate', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.2', 'Choose the word most nearly OPPOSITE in meaning to TRANSPARENT.', '["clear", "translucent", "opaque", "fragile"]', 2, 'Transparent material lets light through clearly; opaque material lets no light through.', '{"0": "Clear is a synonym of transparent.", "1": "Translucent lets some light through, so it is between the two, not the opposite.", "3": "Fragile is about breaking easily."}'),
  ('ENG-0041', 'English', 'Synonyms and antonyms', 'Antonyms', 'easy', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.2', 'Choose the word most nearly OPPOSITE in meaning to CONCISE.', '["brief", "accurate", "clear", "wordy"]', 3, 'Concise means expressing much in few words; wordy is the opposite.', '{"0": "Brief is a synonym.", "1": "Accuracy is a different quality.", "2": "Clarity is a different quality, often linked with conciseness."}'),
  ('ENG-0042', 'English', 'Synonyms and antonyms', 'Antonyms', 'easy', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.2', 'Choose the word most nearly OPPOSITE in meaning to GENEROUS.', '["stingy", "kind", "wealthy", "honest"]', 0, 'Generous means ready to give freely; stingy means unwilling to give or spend.', '{"1": "Kind is close in meaning to generous.", "2": "Wealth is having money, not willingness to give it.", "3": "Honest is an unrelated quality."}'),
  ('ENG-0043', 'English', 'Synonyms and antonyms', 'Antonyms', 'easy', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.2', 'Choose the word most nearly OPPOSITE in meaning to SCARCE.', '["rare", "plentiful", "costly", "empty"]', 1, 'Scarce means in short supply; plentiful is its opposite.', '{"0": "Rare is a synonym.", "2": "Scarce goods are often costly, but costly is not the opposite.", "3": "Empty is not the opposite of scarce."}'),
  ('ENG-0044', 'English', 'Synonyms and antonyms', 'Antonyms', 'easy', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.2', 'Choose the word most nearly OPPOSITE in meaning to OPTIMISTIC.', '["hopeful", "cheerful", "pessimistic", "confident"]', 2, 'An optimist expects good outcomes; a pessimist expects bad ones.', '{"0": "Hopeful is a synonym.", "1": "Cheerful is close in meaning.", "3": "Confident is close in meaning."}'),
  ('ENG-0045', 'English', 'Synonyms and antonyms', 'Antonyms', 'moderate', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.2', 'Choose the word most nearly OPPOSITE in meaning to ACQUIT.', '["release", "pardon", "question", "convict"]', 3, 'To acquit is to declare someone not guilty; to convict is to declare them guilty.', '{"0": "Release is a result of acquittal, not its opposite.", "1": "Pardon also frees a person, so it is not the opposite.", "2": "Questioning comes before a verdict; it is not the opposite."}'),
  ('ENG-0046', 'English', 'Synonyms and antonyms', 'Antonyms', 'easy', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.2', 'Choose the word most nearly OPPOSITE in meaning to DILIGENT.', '["lazy", "hard-working", "careful", "punctual"]', 0, 'Diligent means hard-working; lazy is its opposite.', '{"1": "Hard-working is a synonym.", "2": "Careful is close in meaning.", "3": "Punctual means on time, a different quality."}'),
  ('ENG-0047', 'English', 'Synonyms and antonyms', 'Shades of meaning', 'moderate', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.2', 'Choose the word that best completes the sentence:
The child was not merely hungry; after three days without food, he was ______.', '["peckish", "famished", "full", "thirsty"]', 1, '''Not merely hungry'' calls for a stronger word. Famished means extremely hungry.', '{"0": "Peckish means slightly hungry, weaker than ''hungry''.", "2": "Full is the opposite.", "3": "Thirsty is about water, not food."}'),
  ('ENG-0048', 'English', 'Synonyms and antonyms', 'Shades of meaning', 'moderate', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.2', 'Choose the word that best completes the sentence:
She did not just dislike the proposal; she ______ it.', '["tolerated", "preferred", "loathed", "noticed"]', 2, '''Did not just dislike'' needs a stronger word for the same feeling. Loathe means to hate intensely.', '{"0": "Tolerating is weaker than disliking.", "1": "Preferred is positive, not a stronger dislike.", "3": "Noticed shows no feeling at all."}'),
  ('ENG-0049', 'English', 'Synonyms and antonyms', 'Shades of meaning', 'moderate', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.2', 'Which of these words expresses the STRONGEST degree of anger?', '["annoyance", "irritation", "displeasure", "fury"]', 3, 'All four words name anger, but fury is wild, violent anger, the strongest of the group.', '{"0": "Annoyance is mild anger.", "1": "Irritation is mild anger.", "2": "Displeasure is mild dissatisfaction."}')
) AS s
JOIN users u ON u.email = 'hozaiphaa@gmail.com';

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions
  (id, subject, chapter, topic, difficulty, source, stem, options, answer, why, wrong, stage, free, checks, writer_id, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT s.column1, s.column2, s.column3, s.column4, s.column5, s.column6, s.column7, s.column8, s.column9, s.column10, s.column11,
  'live', 0, '[]', u.id, unixepoch() * 1000, unixepoch() * 1000
FROM (VALUES
  ('ENG-0050', 'English', 'Synonyms and antonyms', 'Shades of meaning', 'hard', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.2', 'Which word describes someone who is careful with money in a NEGATIVE sense?', '["miserly", "thrifty", "economical", "prudent"]', 0, 'All four mean careful with money, but miserly carries a negative shade: unwilling to spend even when one should.', '{"1": "Thrifty has a positive shade.", "2": "Economical is neutral or positive.", "3": "Prudent is positive."}'),
  ('ENG-0051', 'English', 'Synonyms and antonyms', 'Shades of meaning', 'hard', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.2', 'Which word describes a person who keeps to a decision in a POSITIVE sense?', '["stubborn", "determined", "obstinate", "pig-headed"]', 1, 'All four mean holding firmly to a decision, but only determined is praise; the others suggest unreasonable refusal to change.', '{"0": "Stubborn has a negative shade.", "2": "Obstinate has a negative shade.", "3": "Pig-headed is strongly negative."}'),
  ('ENG-0052', 'English', 'Synonyms and antonyms', 'Shades of meaning', 'easy', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.2', 'Choose the word that best completes the sentence:
The old man ______ slowly along the road, leaning heavily on his stick.', '["sprinted", "marched", "hobbled", "skipped"]', 2, 'Hobble means to walk awkwardly, as with pain or weakness, which fits ''slowly'' and ''leaning heavily on his stick''.', '{"0": "Sprinting is running fast.", "1": "Marching is a firm, regular stride.", "3": "Skipping is a light, bouncy movement."}'),
  ('ENG-0053', 'English', 'Synonyms and antonyms', 'Homophones', 'easy', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.2', 'Choose the correct word for the blank:
The doctor''s ______ to stop smoking was ignored by the patient.', '["advice", "advise", "advize", "adviced"]', 0, '''Advice'' (with c) is the noun; ''advise'' (with s) is the verb. After ''the doctor''s'', a noun is needed.', '{"1": "''Advise'' is the verb.", "2": "''Advize'' is not a word.", "3": "''Adviced'' is not a word."}'),
  ('ENG-0054', 'English', 'Synonyms and antonyms', 'Homophones', 'moderate', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.2', 'Choose the correct word for the blank:
Too much salt can ______ your blood pressure.', '["effect", "affect", "afect", "affection"]', 1, '''Affect'' is usually the verb (to influence); ''effect'' is usually the noun (a result).', '{"0": "''Effect'' is normally a noun; as a verb it means ''to bring about''.", "2": "''Afect'' is misspelled.", "3": "''Affection'' means fondness."}'),
  ('ENG-0055', 'English', 'Synonyms and antonyms', 'Homophones', 'easy', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.2', 'Choose the correct word for the blank:
The students left ______ bags outside the exam hall.', '["there", "they''re", "their", "thier"]', 2, '''Their'' is the possessive form; the bags belong to the students.', '{"0": "''There'' refers to a place.", "1": "''They''re'' means ''they are''.", "3": "''Thier'' is misspelled."}'),
  ('ENG-0056', 'English', 'Synonyms and antonyms', 'Homophones', 'easy', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.2', 'Choose the correct word for the blank:
Please do not ______ your admit card on the day of the test.', '["loose", "loss", "lost", "lose"]', 3, '''Lose'' (one o) is the verb meaning to misplace; ''loose'' means not tight.', '{"0": "''Loose'' is an adjective meaning not tight.", "1": "''Loss'' is a noun.", "2": "''Do not'' must be followed by the base form, not ''lost''."}'),
  ('ENG-0057', 'English', 'Reading and figurative language', 'Figures of speech', 'easy', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 1.3', '"Her voice was as smooth as silk." This line uses:', '["a metaphor", "personification", "hyperbole", "a simile"]', 3, 'A simile compares two unlike things using ''like'' or ''as''. Here the voice is compared to silk using ''as ... as''.', '{"0": "A metaphor makes the comparison without ''like'' or ''as''.", "1": "Personification gives human qualities to non-human things.", "2": "Hyperbole is deliberate exaggeration."}'),
  ('ENG-0058', 'English', 'Reading and figurative language', 'Figures of speech', 'easy', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 1.3', '"When the teacher left, the classroom was a zoo." This line uses:', '["a metaphor", "a simile", "onomatopoeia", "alliteration"]', 0, 'A metaphor says one thing IS another. The noisy classroom is called a zoo without ''like'' or ''as''.', '{"1": "There is no ''like'' or ''as'', so it is not a simile.", "2": "Onomatopoeia uses words that imitate sounds.", "3": "Alliteration repeats initial consonant sounds."}'),
  ('ENG-0059', 'English', 'Reading and figurative language', 'Figures of speech', 'easy', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 1.3', '"The wind whispered secrets through the pine trees." This line uses:', '["a simile", "personification", "an oxymoron", "irony"]', 1, 'Whispering secrets is a human action given to the wind, which is personification.', '{"0": "There is no comparison using ''like'' or ''as''.", "2": "An oxymoron joins two contradictory words.", "3": "Irony involves a contrast between what is said and what is meant or expected."}'),
  ('ENG-0060', 'English', 'Reading and figurative language', 'Figures of speech', 'easy', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 1.3', '"I have told you a million times to switch off the fan." This line uses:', '["a simile", "personification", "hyperbole", "onomatopoeia"]', 2, 'Hyperbole is deliberate exaggeration for effect; nobody has literally said it a million times.', '{"0": "No comparison with ''like'' or ''as'' is made.", "1": "Nothing non-human is given human qualities.", "3": "No word imitates a sound."}'),
  ('ENG-0061', 'English', 'Reading and figurative language', 'Figures of speech', 'easy', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 1.3', '"The bees buzzed and the kettle hissed." This line mainly uses:', '["a metaphor", "hyperbole", "an oxymoron", "onomatopoeia"]', 3, 'Buzz and hiss imitate the sounds they describe, which is onomatopoeia.', '{"0": "Nothing is called something else.", "1": "There is no exaggeration.", "2": "No contradictory words are joined."}'),
  ('ENG-0062', 'English', 'Reading and figurative language', 'Figures of speech', 'moderate', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 1.3', '"After the bad news, a deafening silence filled the room." The phrase "deafening silence" is:', '["an oxymoron", "a simile", "alliteration", "onomatopoeia"]', 0, 'An oxymoron joins two words that seem to contradict each other: silence cannot literally be deafening.', '{"1": "No ''like'' or ''as'' comparison is made.", "2": "The two words do not begin with the same consonant sound.", "3": "Neither word imitates a sound."}'),
  ('ENG-0063', 'English', 'Reading and figurative language', 'Figures of speech', 'moderate', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 1.3', '"Silent snow settled softly on the sleeping city." The repeated "s" sound at the start of words is:', '["assonance", "alliteration", "a metaphor", "hyperbole"]', 1, 'Alliteration is the repetition of the same consonant sound at the beginning of nearby words.', '{"0": "Assonance repeats vowel sounds, not initial consonants.", "2": "Nothing is called something else.", "3": "There is no exaggeration."}'),
  ('ENG-0064', 'English', 'Reading and figurative language', 'Figures of speech', 'moderate', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 1.3', 'Your team loses 6-0 and the coach says, "Well, that went brilliantly." The coach''s remark is an example of:', '["a simile", "personification", "verbal irony", "an oxymoron"]', 2, 'Verbal irony is saying the opposite of what one means; the coach means it went very badly.', '{"0": "There is no ''like'' or ''as'' comparison.", "1": "Nothing non-human is given human qualities.", "3": "An oxymoron joins two contradictory words in one phrase."}'),
  ('ENG-0065', 'English', 'Reading and figurative language', 'Figures of speech', 'moderate', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 1.3', 'A fire station burns down because its own smoke alarm was faulty. This is an example of:', '["verbal irony", "a metaphor", "hyperbole", "situational irony"]', 3, 'Situational irony is when the outcome is the opposite of what one would expect; a fire station is the last place one expects a fire to win.', '{"0": "Verbal irony is about what someone says, not what happens.", "1": "Nothing is called something else.", "2": "Nothing is exaggerated."}'),
  ('ENG-0066', 'English', 'Reading and figurative language', 'Irony, parody and satire', 'moderate', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.2', 'A piece of writing that uses humour, exaggeration and ridicule to criticise people''s foolishness or a society''s faults is called:', '["satire", "an elegy", "a eulogy", "an autobiography"]', 0, 'Satire mocks folly or vice in order to criticise and correct it.', '{"1": "An elegy is a sad poem mourning the dead.", "2": "A eulogy is a speech praising someone, usually after death.", "3": "An autobiography is a person''s account of their own life."}'),
  ('ENG-0067', 'English', 'Reading and figurative language', 'Irony, parody and satire', 'moderate', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.2', 'A comic imitation of a well-known poem, copying its style but exaggerating it to make fun of it, is called:', '["a paraphrase", "a parody", "a prologue", "an epic"]', 1, 'A parody imitates the style of a work or author with deliberate exaggeration for comic effect.', '{"0": "A paraphrase restates meaning in other words, without mockery.", "2": "A prologue is an introduction to a play or book.", "3": "An epic is a long serious narrative poem."}'),
  ('ENG-0068', 'English', 'Reading and figurative language', 'Scanning a passage', 'easy', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 1.1', 'Read the passage and answer the question.
Dengue fever is spread by the bite of the female Aedes mosquito, which breeds in clean, still water such as that collected in flower pots, old tyres and water coolers. Unlike the mosquito that spreads malaria, it bites mostly during the day, especially in the early morning and late afternoon. Most patients recover with rest and fluids, but in a small number the platelet count falls sharply. Because there is no specific antiviral cure, prevention depends largely on removing breeding sites.
According to the passage, when does the Aedes mosquito mostly bite?', '["Only late at night", "Only at noon", "During the day, especially early morning and late afternoon", "Throughout the night"]', 2, 'The passage says it ''bites mostly during the day, especially in the early morning and late afternoon''.', '{"0": "The passage contrasts it with night-biting; it says ''during the day''.", "1": "Noon is not mentioned; the peaks are morning and late afternoon.", "3": "The passage says day, not night."}'),
  ('ENG-0069', 'English', 'Reading and figurative language', 'Inference from a passage', 'moderate', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 1.2', 'Read the passage and answer the question.
Dengue fever is spread by the bite of the female Aedes mosquito, which breeds in clean, still water such as that collected in flower pots, old tyres and water coolers. Unlike the mosquito that spreads malaria, it bites mostly during the day, especially in the early morning and late afternoon. Most patients recover with rest and fluids, but in a small number the platelet count falls sharply. Because there is no specific antiviral cure, prevention depends largely on removing breeding sites.
Which statement is best supported by the passage?', '["Dengue is usually cured with antiviral drugs", "Most dengue patients develop a dangerously low platelet count", "The Aedes mosquito breeds mainly in sewage", "Removing stagnant clean water around homes can help prevent dengue"]', 3, 'The passage says the mosquito breeds in clean still water and that prevention depends on removing breeding sites.', '{"0": "The passage says there is no specific antiviral cure.", "1": "Only ''a small number'' have a sharp fall in platelets; most recover.", "2": "It breeds in clean, still water, not sewage."}')
) AS s
JOIN users u ON u.email = 'hozaiphaa@gmail.com';

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions
  (id, subject, chapter, topic, difficulty, source, stem, options, answer, why, wrong, stage, free, checks, writer_id, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT s.column1, s.column2, s.column3, s.column4, s.column5, s.column6, s.column7, s.column8, s.column9, s.column10, s.column11,
  'live', 0, '[]', u.id, unixepoch() * 1000, unixepoch() * 1000
FROM (VALUES
  ('ENG-0070', 'English', 'Reading and figurative language', 'Meaning from context', 'easy', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 1.2', 'Read the passage and answer the question.
Dengue fever is spread by the bite of the female Aedes mosquito, which breeds in clean, still water such as that collected in flower pots, old tyres and water coolers. Unlike the mosquito that spreads malaria, it bites mostly during the day, especially in the early morning and late afternoon. Most patients recover with rest and fluids, but in a small number the platelet count falls sharply. Because there is no specific antiviral cure, prevention depends largely on removing breeding sites.
In the passage, the word "sharply" most nearly means:', '["suddenly and steeply", "painfully", "slightly", "slowly"]', 0, 'A count that ''falls sharply'' drops suddenly and by a large amount.', '{"1": "Sharp can describe pain, but a count cannot fall painfully.", "2": "Slightly is the opposite of sharply here.", "3": "Slowly is the opposite of sharply here."}'),
  ('ENG-0071', 'English', 'Reading and figurative language', 'Main idea', 'easy', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 1.2', 'Read the passage and answer the question.
Researchers have long known that sleep helps the brain store new information. In one study, students who learned a list of words in the evening and then slept recalled more of them the next morning than students who learned the same list in the morning and stayed awake all day before being tested. The researchers concluded that sleep does not merely protect new memories from being disturbed; it actively strengthens them.
What is the main idea of the passage?', '["Morning is the best time to study", "Sleep helps strengthen newly learned information", "Word lists are the best way to learn", "Students should sleep during lectures"]', 1, 'Every sentence supports the claim that sleep helps the brain store and strengthen new memories.', '{"0": "The study actually favoured the group that learned in the evening and then slept.", "2": "Word lists were only the material used in the study.", "3": "The passage says nothing about sleeping in lectures."}'),
  ('ENG-0072', 'English', 'Reading and figurative language', 'Scanning a passage', 'moderate', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 1.1', 'Read the passage and answer the question.
Researchers have long known that sleep helps the brain store new information. In one study, students who learned a list of words in the evening and then slept recalled more of them the next morning than students who learned the same list in the morning and stayed awake all day before being tested. The researchers concluded that sleep does not merely protect new memories from being disturbed; it actively strengthens them.
What did the researchers conclude, beyond the idea that sleep protects memories?', '["Sleep erases unimportant memories", "Staying awake improves recall", "Sleep actively strengthens new memories", "Evening learning is always harder"]', 2, 'The last sentence says sleep ''does not merely protect'' memories; ''it actively strengthens them''.', '{"0": "Erasing memories is not mentioned.", "1": "The group that stayed awake recalled fewer words.", "3": "The evening group did better, not worse."}'),
  ('ENG-0073', 'English', 'Reading and figurative language', 'Tone and irony', 'hard', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 1.3', 'Read the passage and answer the question.
The city''s new ''smart'' parking system, which promised to end traffic jams for ever, has been a triumph. Drivers now spend only twice as long looking for a space as they did before, and the helpful app crashes no more than three times a day.
The tone of the passage is best described as:', '["admiring", "neutral and factual", "sorrowful", "sarcastic"]', 3, 'The writer calls the system ''a triumph'' while listing failures (twice as long, crashes three times a day). Praising what is plainly bad is sarcasm.', '{"0": "The ''praise'' is contradicted by the facts given, so it is not sincere admiration.", "1": "Words like ''triumph'' and ''helpful'' are loaded, not neutral.", "2": "The writer mocks rather than grieves."}'),
  ('ENG-0074', 'English', 'Pronoun-antecedent agreement', 'Indefinite pronouns as antecedents', 'moderate', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.3', 'Choose the option that correctly fills the blank:
Each of the boys must bring ______ own lunch.', '["his", "their", "our", "its"]', 0, '''Each'' is singular and the boys are male, so the pronoun is singular masculine: his.', '{"1": "''Their'' is plural; the subject ''each'' is singular.", "2": "''Our'' would include the speaker, who is not the antecedent.", "3": "''Its'' is for things or animals, not boys."}'),
  ('ENG-0075', 'English', 'Pronoun-antecedent agreement', 'Neither...nor and either...or', 'moderate', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.3', 'Choose the option that correctly fills the blank:
Neither Sara nor Hina remembered to bring ______ calculator.', '["their", "her", "hers", "they"]', 1, 'Two singular antecedents joined by neither...nor take a singular pronoun; both are female, so ''her''.', '{"0": "Neither...nor joining singular nouns takes a singular pronoun.", "2": "''Hers'' stands alone and cannot come before a noun.", "3": "''They'' is a subject pronoun and plural."}'),
  ('ENG-0076', 'English', 'Pronoun-antecedent agreement', 'Neither...nor and either...or', 'hard', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.3', 'Choose the option that correctly fills the blank:
Either the teacher or the students will present ______ findings.', '["his", "her", "their", "its"]', 2, 'With either...or, the pronoun agrees with the nearer antecedent. ''Students'' is plural, so ''their''.', '{"0": "The nearer antecedent ''students'' is plural.", "1": "The nearer antecedent ''students'' is plural.", "3": "''Its'' is for things, and the nearer antecedent is plural."}'),
  ('ENG-0077', 'English', 'Pronoun-antecedent agreement', 'Compound antecedents', 'easy', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.3', 'Choose the option that correctly fills the blank:
Ahmed and Bilal finished ______ project a day early.', '["his", "its", "there", "their"]', 3, 'Two antecedents joined by ''and'' make a plural antecedent, so the pronoun is ''their''.', '{"0": "''His'' refers to one person; the antecedent is two people.", "1": "''Its'' is for things.", "2": "''There'' is an adverb of place, not a possessive."}'),
  ('ENG-0078', 'English', 'Pronoun-antecedent agreement', 'Consistency of person', 'moderate', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.3', 'Choose the option that correctly fills the blank:
One should always keep ______ promises.', '["one''s", "your", "its", "our"]', 0, 'When the antecedent is the impersonal pronoun ''one'', the matching possessive is ''one''s''.', '{"1": "Switching from ''one'' to ''your'' is a shift in person.", "2": "''Its'' is for things.", "3": "Switching from ''one'' to ''our'' is a shift in person."}'),
  ('ENG-0079', 'English', 'Pronoun-antecedent agreement', 'Possessive pronouns', 'easy', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.3', 'Choose the option that correctly fills the blank:
The dog wagged ______ tail when it saw its owner.', '["it''s", "its", "its''", "their"]', 1, '''Its'' (no apostrophe) is the possessive form of ''it''.', '{"0": "''It''s'' means ''it is'' or ''it has''.", "2": "''Its'''' is not an English word.", "3": "''Their'' is plural; the antecedent is one dog."}'),
  ('ENG-0080', 'English', 'Pronoun-antecedent agreement', 'Indefinite pronouns as antecedents', 'easy', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.3', 'Choose the option that correctly fills the blank:
Each of the girls has submitted ______ assignment.', '["their", "hers", "her", "his"]', 2, '''Each'' is singular and refers to girls, so the pronoun is singular feminine: her.', '{"0": "''Their'' is plural; ''each'' is singular.", "1": "''Hers'' cannot come before a noun.", "3": "''His'' does not match girls."}'),
  ('ENG-0081', 'English', 'Pronoun-antecedent agreement', 'Indefinite pronouns as antecedents', 'moderate', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.3', 'Choose the option that correctly fills the blank:
One of the girls left ______ bag on the bus.', '["their", "its", "his", "her"]', 3, 'The subject is ''one'' (singular), referring to a girl, so ''her''.', '{"0": "The antecedent is ''one'', not ''girls''.", "1": "''Its'' is for things.", "2": "''His'' does not match a girl."}'),
  ('ENG-0082', 'English', 'Pronoun-antecedent agreement', 'Clear pronoun reference', 'hard', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.3', 'Which sentence has an UNCLEAR pronoun reference?', '["Ayesha told Sana that she had won the prize.", "Ayesha told Sana, \"You have won the prize.\"", "When Ayesha won the prize, she told Sana.", "Ayesha won the prize and told Sana about it."]', 0, 'In the first sentence ''she'' could mean either Ayesha or Sana, so the reader cannot tell who won.', '{"1": "''You'' clearly means Sana.", "2": "''She'' can only be Ayesha, the subject of the first clause.", "3": "Ayesha is clearly the winner; ''it'' refers to winning the prize."}'),
  ('ENG-0083', 'English', 'Pronoun-antecedent agreement', 'Relative pronouns', 'easy', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.3', 'Choose the option that correctly fills the blank:
The doctor ______ treated my father has twenty years of experience.', '["which", "who", "whom", "whose"]', 1, 'The relative pronoun stands for a person and is the subject of ''treated'', so ''who''.', '{"0": "''Which'' is for things, not people.", "2": "''Whom'' is an object form; here the pronoun is the subject of ''treated''.", "3": "''Whose'' shows possession."}'),
  ('ENG-0084', 'English', 'Pronoun-antecedent agreement', 'Relative pronouns', 'moderate', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.3', 'Choose the option that correctly fills the blank:
This is the book ______ cover was torn.', '["who''s", "which", "whose", "whom"]', 2, '''Whose'' is the possessive relative pronoun: the cover belongs to the book.', '{"0": "''Who''s'' means ''who is'' or ''who has''.", "1": "''Which cover was torn'' would need a different structure.", "3": "''Whom'' is an object pronoun for people."}'),
  ('ENG-0085', 'English', 'Pronoun-antecedent agreement', 'Pronoun case', 'moderate', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.3', 'Choose the option that correctly fills the blank:
My brother is taller than ______ am.', '["me", "myself", "mine", "I"]', 3, 'The verb ''am'' follows, so a subject pronoun is needed: ''than I am''.', '{"0": "''Me am'' is ungrammatical; ''me'' is an object form.", "1": "Reflexive ''myself'' cannot be a subject.", "2": "''Mine'' is possessive."}'),
  ('ENG-0086', 'English', 'Pronoun-antecedent agreement', 'Reflexive pronouns', 'easy', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.3', 'Choose the option that correctly fills the blank:
Hamza hurt ______ while playing cricket.', '["himself", "hisself", "him", "themself"]', 0, 'When the doer and receiver are the same person, a reflexive pronoun is used: himself.', '{"1": "''Hisself'' is not standard English.", "2": "''Him'' would suggest another person was hurt.", "3": "The antecedent is one male person; ''himself'' is the standard form."}'),
  ('ENG-0087', 'English', 'Pronoun-antecedent agreement', 'Compound antecedents', 'easy', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.3', 'Choose the option that correctly fills the blank:
Both the cars had ______ tyres changed.', '["its", "their", "it''s", "there"]', 1, '''Both the cars'' is plural, so the plural possessive ''their'' is needed.', '{"0": "''Its'' is singular.", "2": "''It''s'' means ''it is''.", "3": "''There'' is not a possessive."}'),
  ('ENG-0088', 'English', 'Pronoun-antecedent agreement', 'Pronoun case', 'moderate', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.3', 'Choose the option that correctly fills the blank:
Between you and ______, the plan is unlikely to succeed.', '["I", "myself", "me", "mine"]', 2, '''Between'' is a preposition, and a pronoun after a preposition takes the object form: me.', '{"0": "''I'' is a subject form; it cannot follow a preposition.", "1": "''Myself'' needs an earlier ''I'' in the same clause.", "3": "''Mine'' is possessive."}'),
  ('ENG-0089', 'English', 'Tenses', 'Present perfect', 'easy', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.4', 'Choose the option that correctly fills the blank:
She ______ in Lahore since 2015.', '["lives", "is living", "lived", "has lived"]', 3, '''Since'' plus a starting point describes an action that began in the past and continues now: present perfect.', '{"0": "Simple present does not go with ''since 2015''.", "1": "Present continuous does not go with ''since 2015''.", "2": "Simple past would mean she no longer lives there, which clashes with ''since''."}')
) AS s
JOIN users u ON u.email = 'hozaiphaa@gmail.com';

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions
  (id, subject, chapter, topic, difficulty, source, stem, options, answer, why, wrong, stage, free, checks, writer_id, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT s.column1, s.column2, s.column3, s.column4, s.column5, s.column6, s.column7, s.column8, s.column9, s.column10, s.column11,
  'live', 0, '[]', u.id, unixepoch() * 1000, unixepoch() * 1000
FROM (VALUES
  ('ENG-0090', 'English', 'Tenses', 'Past perfect', 'moderate', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.4', 'Choose the option that correctly fills the blank:
By the time we reached the station, the train ______.', '["had left", "left", "has left", "leaves"]', 0, 'Of two past actions, the earlier one (the train leaving) takes the past perfect.', '{"1": "Simple past does not show that leaving happened before we reached.", "2": "Present perfect cannot be used inside a past time frame.", "3": "Simple present does not fit a past narrative."}'),
  ('ENG-0091', 'English', 'Tenses', 'Conditionals', 'easy', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.4', 'Choose the option that correctly fills the blank:
If it rains tomorrow, we ______ the match.', '["would postpone", "will postpone", "postponed", "had postponed"]', 1, 'A real future condition (first conditional) uses present simple in the if-clause and ''will'' in the main clause.', '{"0": "''Would'' belongs to an unreal (second) conditional with a past-tense if-clause.", "2": "Past tense does not fit ''tomorrow''.", "3": "Past perfect is for the third conditional."}'),
  ('ENG-0092', 'English', 'Tenses', 'Simple present', 'easy', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.4', 'Choose the option that correctly fills the blank:
Water ______ at 100 °C at sea level.', '["is boiling", "boiled", "boils", "has boiled"]', 2, 'General scientific truths take the simple present.', '{"0": "Present continuous describes something happening now, not a general fact.", "1": "Past tense does not express a permanent truth.", "3": "Present perfect does not express a general truth."}'),
  ('ENG-0093', 'English', 'Tenses', 'Past continuous', 'easy', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.4', 'Choose the option that correctly fills the blank:
I ______ my homework when the lights went out.', '["am doing", "have done", "will do", "was doing"]', 3, 'An action in progress in the past, interrupted by a short event, takes the past continuous.', '{"0": "Present continuous does not match the past ''went out''.", "1": "Present perfect does not fit a specific past moment.", "2": "Future does not match a past event."}'),
  ('ENG-0094', 'English', 'Tenses', 'Present continuous', 'easy', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.4', 'Choose the option that correctly fills the blank:
Look! The baby ______.', '["is sleeping", "sleeps", "slept", "has slept"]', 0, '''Look!'' points to an action happening right now, so the present continuous is used.', '{"1": "Simple present describes habits, not what is happening now.", "2": "Past tense does not fit ''Look!''.", "3": "Present perfect shows a completed action, not one in progress."}'),
  ('ENG-0095', 'English', 'Tenses', 'Future perfect', 'moderate', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.4', 'Choose the option that correctly fills the blank:
By next June, she ______ her house job.', '["completes", "will have completed", "has completed", "had completed"]', 1, 'An action finished before a stated future time takes the future perfect.', '{"0": "Simple present does not show completion before a future point.", "2": "Present perfect cannot point to a future deadline.", "3": "Past perfect is for before a past time."}'),
  ('ENG-0096', 'English', 'Tenses', 'Past perfect continuous', 'hard', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.4', 'Choose the option that correctly fills the blank:
He ______ for three hours when the doctor finally saw him.', '["has been waiting", "is waiting", "had been waiting", "will have been waiting"]', 2, 'An action that continued over a period up to a past moment takes the past perfect continuous.', '{"0": "Present perfect continuous connects to now, but the story is in the past (''saw'').", "1": "Present continuous does not fit a past event.", "3": "Future perfect continuous points to the future."}'),
  ('ENG-0097', 'English', 'Tenses', 'Simple past vs present perfect', 'moderate', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.4', 'Which sentence is grammatically correct?', '["I have visited Murree last year.", "I had visited Murree last year.", "I have been visiting Murree last year.", "I visited Murree last year."]', 3, 'A finished action at a stated past time (''last year'') takes the simple past.', '{"0": "Present perfect cannot be used with a finished past time like ''last year''.", "1": "Past perfect needs another past event to come before.", "2": "Present perfect continuous cannot be used with ''last year''."}'),
  ('ENG-0098', 'English', 'Tenses', 'Simple present', 'easy', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.4', 'Choose the option that correctly fills the blank:
Ali ______ to college every day.', '["goes", "go", "going", "gone"]', 0, 'A habit with a third-person singular subject takes the simple present with -s: goes.', '{"1": "''Go'' does not agree with the singular subject ''Ali''.", "2": "''Going'' needs a helping verb.", "3": "''Gone'' is a past participle and needs ''has''."}'),
  ('ENG-0099', 'English', 'Tenses', 'Past perfect', 'moderate', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.4', 'Choose the option that correctly fills the blank:
When I arrived, they ______, so there was nothing left.', '["have already eaten", "had already eaten", "are already eating", "will already eat"]', 1, 'Eating happened before my arrival in the past, so the past perfect is used.', '{"0": "Present perfect does not fit a past story.", "2": "Present continuous does not fit a past story.", "3": "Future does not fit a past story."}'),
  ('ENG-0100', 'English', 'Tenses', 'Stative verbs', 'moderate', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.4', 'Choose the option that correctly fills the blank:
I ______ the answer now.', '["am knowing", "have been knowing", "know", "was knowing"]', 2, '''Know'' is a stative verb, so it is normally not used in continuous forms.', '{"0": "Stative verbs like ''know'' do not take the continuous form.", "1": "Stative verbs like ''know'' do not take the continuous form.", "3": "Past continuous does not fit ''now'', and ''know'' is stative."}'),
  ('ENG-0101', 'English', 'Tenses', 'Present perfect', 'hard', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.4', 'Choose the option that correctly fills the blank:
This ______ the third time you have been late this week.', '["was", "has been", "will be", "is"]', 3, 'The pattern ''This is the first/second/third time + present perfect'' is fixed.', '{"0": "''Was'' does not match the present perfect ''have been''.", "1": "''Has been'' is not used in this fixed pattern.", "2": "''Will be'' points to the future, but the lateness has already happened."}'),
  ('ENG-0102', 'English', 'Tenses', 'Present perfect', 'moderate', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.4', 'Choose the option that correctly fills the blank:
The patient ______ anything since he was admitted last night.', '["has not eaten", "did not eat", "does not eat", "is not eating"]', 0, '''Since'' a past point up to now takes the present perfect.', '{"1": "Simple past does not link to ''since'' up to now.", "2": "Simple present describes habits.", "3": "Present continuous does not go with ''since''."}'),
  ('ENG-0103', 'English', 'Tenses', 'Future continuous', 'moderate', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.4', 'Choose the option that correctly fills the blank:
This time tomorrow, I ______ over the Arabian Sea.', '["flew", "will be flying", "have flown", "had been flying"]', 1, 'An action in progress at a particular future time takes the future continuous.', '{"0": "Past tense does not fit ''tomorrow''.", "2": "Present perfect does not fit a future time.", "3": "Past perfect continuous refers to the past."}'),
  ('ENG-0104', 'English', 'Tenses', 'Future time clauses', 'moderate', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.4', 'Choose the option that correctly fills the blank:
I will call you as soon as I ______ home.', '["will reach", "reached", "reach", "would reach"]', 2, 'In time clauses (as soon as, when, after) about the future, English uses the simple present, not ''will''.', '{"0": "''Will'' is not used inside a future time clause.", "1": "Past tense does not fit a future plan.", "3": "''Would'' does not fit a real future plan."}'),
  ('ENG-0105', 'English', 'Tenses', 'Present perfect continuous', 'moderate', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.4', 'Choose the option that correctly fills the blank:
How long ______ in this hospital? You must know everyone here by now.', '["are you working", "do you work", "were you working", "have you been working"]', 3, '''How long'' about an activity that started in the past and is still going on takes the present perfect continuous.', '{"0": "Present continuous does not show duration up to now.", "1": "Simple present does not show duration up to now.", "2": "Past continuous suggests the job is over, but ''by now'' shows it continues."}'),
  ('ENG-0106', 'English', 'Tenses', 'Conditionals', 'moderate', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.4', 'Choose the option that correctly fills the blank:
If I ______ the answer, I would tell you.', '["knew", "know", "will know", "had know"]', 0, 'An unreal present condition (second conditional) uses the past simple in the if-clause and ''would'' in the main clause.', '{"1": "Present simple belongs with ''will'' (first conditional).", "2": "''Will'' is not used in the if-clause.", "3": "''Had know'' is not a verb form."}'),
  ('ENG-0107', 'English', 'Tenses', 'Conditionals', 'hard', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.4', 'Choose the option that correctly fills the blank:
If she had taken her medicine on time, she ______ so ill.', '["would not become", "would not have become", "will not become", "did not become"]', 1, 'An unreal past condition (third conditional) uses ''had + past participle'' in the if-clause and ''would have + past participle'' in the main clause.', '{"0": "This is a second-conditional main clause.", "2": "''Will'' belongs to the first conditional.", "3": "The simple past does not fit an imagined past result."}'),
  ('ENG-0108', 'English', 'Tenses', 'Conditionals', 'easy', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.4', 'Choose the option that correctly fills the blank:
If you heat ice, it ______.', '["melted", "would melt", "melts", "had melted"]', 2, 'A general truth or scientific fact uses the zero conditional: present simple in both clauses.', '{"0": "Past simple does not state a general truth.", "1": "''Would'' suggests an unreal situation.", "3": "Past perfect does not fit a general truth."}'),
  ('ENG-0109', 'English', 'Infinitives and gerunds', 'Verbs followed by a gerund', 'easy', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.6', 'Choose the option that correctly fills the blank:
She enjoys ______ detective novels.', '["reading", "to read", "read", "to reading"]', 0, '''Enjoy'' is followed by a gerund (-ing form).', '{"1": "''Enjoy'' does not take a to-infinitive.", "2": "A bare verb cannot follow ''enjoy''.", "3": "''To reading'' is not correct after ''enjoy''."}')
) AS s
JOIN users u ON u.email = 'hozaiphaa@gmail.com';

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions
  (id, subject, chapter, topic, difficulty, source, stem, options, answer, why, wrong, stage, free, checks, writer_id, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT s.column1, s.column2, s.column3, s.column4, s.column5, s.column6, s.column7, s.column8, s.column9, s.column10, s.column11,
  'live', 0, '[]', u.id, unixepoch() * 1000, unixepoch() * 1000
FROM (VALUES
  ('ENG-0110', 'English', 'Infinitives and gerunds', 'Verbs followed by an infinitive', 'easy', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.5', 'Choose the option that correctly fills the blank:
He decided ______ medicine after his uncle''s illness.', '["studying", "to study", "study", "to studying"]', 1, '''Decide'' is followed by a to-infinitive.', '{"0": "''Decide'' does not take a gerund.", "2": "A bare infinitive cannot follow ''decide''.", "3": "''To studying'' mixes the infinitive marker with a gerund."}'),
  ('ENG-0111', 'English', 'Infinitives and gerunds', 'Gerund after a preposition', 'moderate', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.6', 'Choose the option that correctly fills the blank:
I am looking forward to ______ you at the conference.', '["meet", "to meet", "meeting", "met"]', 2, 'In ''look forward to'', ''to'' is a preposition, so it is followed by a gerund.', '{"0": "A preposition cannot be followed by a bare verb.", "1": "''To'' here is a preposition, not part of an infinitive.", "3": "A past form does not follow a preposition."}'),
  ('ENG-0112', 'English', 'Infinitives and gerunds', 'Bare infinitive', 'moderate', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.5', 'Choose the option that correctly fills the blank:
My parents made me ______ the dishes.', '["to wash", "washing", "washed", "wash"]', 3, 'In the active voice, ''make'' + object is followed by a bare infinitive (without ''to'').', '{"0": "''Made me to wash'' is incorrect in the active voice.", "1": "''Make'' + object does not take a gerund here.", "2": "A past form does not follow ''made me''."}'),
  ('ENG-0113', 'English', 'Infinitives and gerunds', 'Change of meaning: gerund or infinitive', 'hard', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.6', 'Choose the option that correctly fills the blank:
He stopped ______ after the doctor warned him about his lungs.', '["smoking", "to smoke", "smoke", "smoked"]', 0, '''Stop + gerund'' means to quit an activity, which fits the doctor''s warning about his lungs.', '{"1": "''Stopped to smoke'' means he paused in order to smoke, the opposite meaning.", "2": "A bare verb does not follow ''stopped''.", "3": "A past form does not follow ''stopped'' here."}'),
  ('ENG-0114', 'English', 'Infinitives and gerunds', 'Identifying gerunds', 'easy', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.6', 'In the sentence "Swimming is excellent exercise", which word is a gerund?', '["is", "Swimming", "excellent", "exercise"]', 1, '''Swimming'' is an -ing verb form used as a noun (the subject), so it is a gerund.', '{"0": "''Is'' is the main verb.", "2": "''Excellent'' is an adjective.", "3": "''Exercise'' is an ordinary noun, not a verb form."}'),
  ('ENG-0115', 'English', 'Infinitives and gerunds', 'Functions of the infinitive', 'hard', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.5', 'In "She came to Lahore to learn English", the infinitive phrase "to learn English" works as:', '["the subject of the sentence", "an adjective describing Lahore", "an adverb showing purpose", "the main verb"]', 2, 'It tells why she came, so it modifies the verb ''came'' as an adverb of purpose.', '{"0": "The subject is ''She''.", "1": "It does not describe Lahore; it gives a reason for coming.", "3": "The main verb is ''came''."}'),
  ('ENG-0116', 'English', 'Infinitives and gerunds', 'Functions of the infinitive', 'moderate', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.5', 'In "To err is human", the infinitive "To err" works as:', '["an adverb", "an adjective", "the object", "the subject (a noun)"]', 3, '''To err'' is what the sentence is about; it is the subject and functions as a noun.', '{"0": "It does not modify a verb.", "1": "It does not describe a noun.", "2": "It comes before the verb as the subject, not after it as an object."}'),
  ('ENG-0117', 'English', 'Infinitives and gerunds', 'Functions of the infinitive', 'hard', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.5', 'In "I have a lot of work to do", the infinitive "to do" works as:', '["an adjective describing ''work''", "an adverb of purpose", "the subject", "a gerund"]', 0, '''To do'' tells us which work, describing the noun ''work'', so it acts as an adjective.', '{"1": "It does not say why something was done.", "2": "The subject is ''I''.", "3": "A gerund ends in -ing; this is an infinitive."}'),
  ('ENG-0118', 'English', 'Infinitives and gerunds', 'Gerund after a preposition', 'moderate', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.6', 'Choose the option that correctly fills the blank:
She is used to ______ up early for her ward rounds.', '["get", "getting", "got", "gets"]', 1, '''Be used to'' (be accustomed to) contains the preposition ''to'', so it takes a gerund.', '{"0": "''Be used to'' needs a gerund, not a bare verb.", "2": "A past form cannot follow ''to'' here.", "3": "A present -s form cannot follow ''to''."}'),
  ('ENG-0119', 'English', 'Infinitives and gerunds', 'Verbs followed by a gerund', 'moderate', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.6', 'Choose the option that correctly fills the blank:
It is no use ______ about the result now; the paper is over.', '["to worry", "worry", "worrying", "worried"]', 2, '''It is no use'' is followed by a gerund.', '{"0": "The fixed expression is ''it is no use + -ing''.", "1": "A bare verb does not follow ''no use''.", "3": "A past form does not fit."}'),
  ('ENG-0120', 'English', 'Infinitives and gerunds', 'Bare infinitive', 'moderate', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.5', 'Which sentence is grammatically correct?', '["He let me to use his car.", "He let me using his car.", "He let me used his car.", "He let me use his car."]', 3, '''Let'' + object is followed by a bare infinitive.', '{"0": "''Let'' never takes ''to'' before the next verb.", "1": "''Let'' + object does not take a gerund.", "2": "A past form cannot follow ''let me''."}'),
  ('ENG-0121', 'English', 'Infinitives and gerunds', 'Change of meaning: gerund or infinitive', 'hard', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.5', 'Choose the option that correctly fills the blank:
Remember ______ the door when you leave the lab.', '["to lock", "locking", "lock", "locked"]', 0, '''Remember + to-infinitive'' is about a duty still to be done, which fits ''when you leave''.', '{"1": "''Remember locking'' means recalling something already done.", "2": "A bare verb does not follow ''remember''.", "3": "A past form does not fit."}'),
  ('ENG-0122', 'English', 'Infinitives and gerunds', 'Gerund phrases', 'hard', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.6', 'In which sentence is the gerund phrase the object of a preposition?', '["She wants to solve puzzles.", "She is good at solving puzzles.", "She is solving a puzzle.", "The solved puzzle lay on the table."]', 1, '''Solving puzzles'' follows the preposition ''at'' and acts as a noun, so it is a gerund phrase used as the object of a preposition.', '{"0": "''To solve puzzles'' is an infinitive phrase.", "2": "''Is solving'' is a present continuous verb, not a gerund.", "3": "''Solved'' is a past participle used as an adjective."}'),
  ('ENG-0123', 'English', 'Infinitives and gerunds', 'Verbs followed by a gerund', 'easy', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.6', 'Choose the option that correctly fills the blank:
Would you mind ______ the window? It is very hot.', '["to open", "open", "opening", "opened"]', 2, '''Mind'' (would you mind) is followed by a gerund.', '{"0": "''Mind'' does not take a to-infinitive.", "1": "A bare verb does not follow ''mind''.", "3": "A past form does not fit a request."}'),
  ('ENG-0124', 'English', 'Infinitives and gerunds', 'Verbs followed by a gerund', 'easy', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.6', 'Choose the option that correctly fills the blank:
She avoided ______ the question about her result.', '["to answer", "answer", "answered", "answering"]', 3, '''Avoid'' is followed by a gerund.', '{"0": "''Avoid'' does not take a to-infinitive.", "1": "A bare verb does not follow ''avoid''.", "2": "A past form does not fit."}'),
  ('ENG-0125', 'English', 'Adverbs', 'Kinds of adverbs', 'easy', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.7', 'In "The surgeon stitched the wound carefully.", the adverb "carefully" is an adverb of:', '["manner", "place", "time", "frequency"]', 0, '''Carefully'' tells how the surgeon stitched, so it is an adverb of manner.', '{"1": "An adverb of place tells WHERE.", "2": "An adverb of time tells WHEN.", "3": "An adverb of frequency tells HOW OFTEN."}'),
  ('ENG-0126', 'English', 'Adverbs', 'Kinds of adverbs', 'easy', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.7', 'In "We will discuss the results tomorrow.", the adverb "tomorrow" is an adverb of:', '["manner", "time", "place", "frequency"]', 1, '''Tomorrow'' tells when, so it is an adverb of time.', '{"0": "An adverb of manner tells HOW something is done.", "2": "An adverb of place tells WHERE.", "3": "An adverb of frequency tells HOW OFTEN."}'),
  ('ENG-0127', 'English', 'Adverbs', 'Kinds of adverbs', 'easy', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.7', 'In "He seldom eats fast food.", the adverb "seldom" is an adverb of:', '["manner", "place", "frequency", "time"]', 2, '''Seldom'' tells how often, so it is an adverb of frequency.', '{"0": "An adverb of manner tells HOW something is done.", "1": "An adverb of place tells WHERE.", "3": "An adverb of time tells WHEN."}'),
  ('ENG-0128', 'English', 'Adverbs', 'Kinds of adverbs', 'moderate', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.7', 'In "The tea is too hot to drink.", the adverb "too" is an adverb of:', '["manner", "place", "time", "degree"]', 3, '''Too'' tells to what extent the tea is hot, so it is an adverb of degree.', '{"0": "An adverb of manner tells HOW something is done.", "1": "An adverb of place tells WHERE.", "2": "An adverb of time tells WHEN."}'),
  ('ENG-0129', 'English', 'Adverbs', 'Kinds of adverbs', 'easy', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.7', 'In "The children were playing outside.", the adverb "outside" is an adverb of:', '["place", "manner", "time", "frequency"]', 0, '''Outside'' tells where they were playing, so it is an adverb of place.', '{"1": "An adverb of manner tells HOW something is done.", "2": "An adverb of time tells WHEN.", "3": "An adverb of frequency tells HOW OFTEN."}')
) AS s
JOIN users u ON u.email = 'hozaiphaa@gmail.com';

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions
  (id, subject, chapter, topic, difficulty, source, stem, options, answer, why, wrong, stage, free, checks, writer_id, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT s.column1, s.column2, s.column3, s.column4, s.column5, s.column6, s.column7, s.column8, s.column9, s.column10, s.column11,
  'live', 0, '[]', u.id, unixepoch() * 1000, unixepoch() * 1000
FROM (VALUES
  ('ENG-0130', 'English', 'Adverbs', 'Position of adverbs', 'easy', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.7', 'Which sentence places the adverb correctly?', '["Never he is late for class.", "He is never late for class.", "He is late never for class.", "He never late is for class."]', 1, 'An adverb of frequency normally comes after the verb ''be'' (is, are, was).', '{"0": "A frequency adverb does not come before the subject in a normal statement.", "2": "''Never'' cannot come after the complement here.", "3": "The word order of verb and complement is broken."}'),
  ('ENG-0131', 'English', 'Adverbs', 'Position of adverbs', 'moderate', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.7', 'Which sentence places the adverb correctly?', '["She arrives always on time.", "She always on time arrives.", "She always arrives on time.", "Always she on time arrives."]', 2, 'An adverb of frequency normally comes before the main verb (but after ''be'').', '{"0": "''Always'' should come before a main verb like ''arrives''.", "1": "The verb cannot come after ''on time''.", "3": "The word order is broken."}'),
  ('ENG-0132', 'English', 'Adverbs', 'Order of adverbs', 'moderate', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.7', 'Choose the correct order:
She sang ______.', '["last night beautifully at the concert", "at the concert last night beautifully", "last night at the concert beautifully", "beautifully at the concert last night"]', 3, 'When adverbs of manner, place and time come together after the verb, the usual order is manner, then place, then time.', '{"0": "Time should come last, not first.", "1": "Manner should come first, not last.", "2": "This reverses the usual order."}'),
  ('ENG-0133', 'English', 'Adverbs', 'Position of adverbs', 'easy', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.7', 'Which sentence uses ''enough'' correctly?', '["She is old enough to drive.", "She is enough old to drive.", "She is old to drive enough.", "Enough she is old to drive."]', 0, 'As an adverb of degree, ''enough'' comes AFTER the adjective it modifies: old enough.', '{"1": "''Enough'' goes after the adjective, not before it.", "2": "''Enough'' must stay next to ''old''.", "3": "''Enough'' cannot open this sentence."}'),
  ('ENG-0134', 'English', 'Adverbs', 'Position changes meaning', 'moderate', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.7', 'Which sentence means that NOBODY EXCEPT Ali solved the problem?', '["Ali only solved the problem.", "Only Ali solved the problem.", "Ali solved only the problem.", "Ali solved the only problem."]', 1, '''Only'' limits the word it is placed next to. ''Only Ali'' means Ali and no one else.', '{"0": "Here ''only'' limits the action (he merely solved it).", "2": "Here ''only'' limits what he solved.", "3": "Here ''only'' is an adjective: there was just one problem."}'),
  ('ENG-0135', 'English', 'Adverbs', 'Adjective or adverb', 'easy', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.7', 'Choose the option that correctly fills the blank:
He drives very ______ in the rain.', '["careful", "care", "carefully", "carefulness"]', 2, 'An adverb is needed to describe the verb ''drives'': carefully.', '{"0": "''Careful'' is an adjective; it cannot describe the verb.", "1": "''Care'' is a noun or verb.", "3": "''Carefulness'' is a noun."}'),
  ('ENG-0136', 'English', 'Adverbs', 'Adjective or adverb', 'moderate', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.7', 'Choose the option that correctly fills the blank:
She works ______; she rarely takes a day off.', '["hardly", "hardy", "hardness", "hard"]', 3, '''Hard'' is itself an adverb meaning with great effort.', '{"0": "''Hardly'' means ''almost not'', the opposite meaning.", "1": "''Hardy'' is an adjective meaning tough.", "2": "''Hardness'' is a noun."}'),
  ('ENG-0137', 'English', 'Adverbs', 'Adjective or adverb', 'moderate', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.7', 'Choose the option that correctly fills the blank:
I can ______ hear you; please speak louder.', '["hardly", "hard", "hardest", "harder"]', 0, '''Hardly'' means ''almost not'', which fits a request to speak louder.', '{"1": "''Can hard hear'' is ungrammatical.", "2": "A superlative does not fit.", "3": "A comparative does not fit."}'),
  ('ENG-0138', 'English', 'Adverbs', 'Kinds of adverbs', 'easy', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.7', 'Which of these words is an adverb of place?', '["yesterday", "everywhere", "quickly", "often"]', 1, '''Everywhere'' tells where, so it is an adverb of place.', '{"0": "''Yesterday'' is an adverb of time.", "2": "''Quickly'' is an adverb of manner.", "3": "''Often'' is an adverb of frequency."}'),
  ('ENG-0139', 'English', 'Adverbs', 'Adverbs of degree', 'moderate', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.7', 'Which sentence is correct?', '["The tea is too much hot to drink.", "The tea is very hot to drink.", "The tea is too hot to drink.", "The tea is so hot to drink."]', 2, '''Too + adjective + to-infinitive'' shows that something is more than enough to prevent an action.', '{"0": "''Too much'' goes with nouns, not adjectives.", "1": "''Very'' does not carry the idea of preventing the action.", "3": "''So'' needs ''that'' (so hot that...)."}'),
  ('ENG-0140', 'English', 'Adverbs', 'Kinds of adverbs', 'moderate', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.7', 'In "He arrived late for the viva", the word "late" is:', '["an adjective", "a noun", "a preposition", "an adverb of time"]', 3, '''Late'' modifies the verb ''arrived'', telling when, so here it is an adverb.', '{"0": "As an adjective it would describe a noun (a late train).", "1": "It does not name anything.", "2": "It does not link a noun to the rest of the sentence."}'),
  ('ENG-0141', 'English', 'Prepositions', 'Prepositions of time', 'easy', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.8', 'Choose the option that correctly fills the blank:
The anatomy exam starts ______ 9 a.m.', '["in", "on", "by", "at"]', 3, '''At'' is used for a precise clock time.', '{"0": "''In'' is for months, years and parts of the day.", "1": "''On'' is for days and dates.", "2": "''By'' means ''not later than'', which changes the meaning."}'),
  ('ENG-0142', 'English', 'Prepositions', 'Prepositions of time', 'easy', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.8', 'Choose the option that correctly fills the blank:
She was born ______ 2008.', '["in", "on", "at", "since"]', 0, '''In'' is used with years, months and seasons.', '{"1": "''On'' is for days and dates.", "2": "''At'' is for clock times.", "3": "''Since'' needs a perfect tense and a continuing action."}'),
  ('ENG-0143', 'English', 'Prepositions', 'Prepositions of time', 'easy', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.8', 'Choose the option that correctly fills the blank:
We will meet ______ Monday.', '["in", "on", "at", "for"]', 1, '''On'' is used with days and dates.', '{"0": "''In'' is for longer periods like months or years.", "2": "''At'' is for clock times.", "3": "''For'' shows a length of time."}'),
  ('ENG-0144', 'English', 'Prepositions', 'Prepositions of movement', 'easy', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.8', 'Choose the option that correctly fills the blank:
She walked ______ the room and sat down.', '["at", "on", "into", "of"]', 2, '''Into'' shows movement from outside to inside.', '{"0": "''At'' shows a point, not entry.", "1": "''On'' shows a surface.", "3": "''Of'' does not show movement."}'),
  ('ENG-0145', 'English', 'Prepositions', 'Since and for', 'easy', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.8', 'Choose the option that correctly fills the blank:
He has been ill ______ Monday.', '["for", "at", "by", "since"]', 3, '''Since'' is used with a starting point in time and a perfect tense.', '{"0": "''For'' is used with a length of time (for three days).", "1": "''At'' is for clock times.", "2": "''By'' means ''not later than''."}'),
  ('ENG-0146', 'English', 'Prepositions', 'Since and for', 'easy', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.8', 'Choose the option that correctly fills the blank:
She has been revising ______ three hours.', '["for", "since", "from", "at"]', 0, '''For'' is used with a period of time.', '{"1": "''Since'' needs a starting point (since 6 o''clock), not a duration.", "2": "''From'' needs a matching ''to'' or ''till''.", "3": "''At'' is for a point of time."}'),
  ('ENG-0147', 'English', 'Prepositions', 'Prepositions of movement', 'moderate', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.8', 'Choose the option that correctly fills the blank:
We swam ______ the river to the other bank.', '["along", "across", "among", "between"]', 1, '''Across'' means from one side to the other.', '{"0": "''Along'' means following the length, not reaching the other bank.", "2": "''Among'' is used with a group of people or things.", "3": "''Between'' needs two things named."}'),
  ('ENG-0148', 'English', 'Prepositions', 'Prepositions of position', 'moderate', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.8', 'Choose the option that correctly fills the blank:
The prize money was divided ______ all the members of the team.', '["between", "within", "among", "along"]', 2, '''Among'' is used when sharing out within a group of more than two.', '{"0": "''Between'' is traditionally used for two people or things.", "1": "''Within'' means inside limits, not sharing.", "3": "''Along'' shows direction of movement."}'),
  ('ENG-0149', 'English', 'Prepositions', 'Prepositions of position', 'easy', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.8', 'Choose the option that correctly fills the blank:
The mosque stands ______ the bank and the post office.', '["among", "across", "beside of", "between"]', 3, '''Between'' is used with two named things.', '{"0": "''Among'' is for groups of more than two.", "1": "''Across'' means on the other side, not in the middle of two things.", "2": "''Beside of'' is not standard English."}')
) AS s
JOIN users u ON u.email = 'hozaiphaa@gmail.com';

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions
  (id, subject, chapter, topic, difficulty, source, stem, options, answer, why, wrong, stage, free, checks, writer_id, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT s.column1, s.column2, s.column3, s.column4, s.column5, s.column6, s.column7, s.column8, s.column9, s.column10, s.column11,
  'live', 0, '[]', u.id, unixepoch() * 1000, unixepoch() * 1000
FROM (VALUES
  ('ENG-0150', 'English', 'Prepositions', 'Prepositions of position', 'moderate', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.8', 'Choose the option that correctly fills the blank:
The train arrived ______ Karachi at noon.', '["in", "at", "to", "on"]', 0, 'We arrive IN a city or country, and AT a smaller place such as a station or airport.', '{"1": "''At'' is for smaller places such as a station.", "2": "''Arrive'' is never followed by ''to''.", "3": "''On'' is used for surfaces or days."}'),
  ('ENG-0151', 'English', 'Prepositions', 'Prepositions of movement', 'easy', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.8', 'Choose the option that correctly fills the blank:
We travelled to Murree ______ bus.', '["on", "by", "with", "in"]', 1, '''By'' is used for means of transport without an article: by bus, by train.', '{"0": "''On'' needs an article: on the bus.", "2": "''With'' is not used for means of transport.", "3": "''In'' needs an article: in a car."}'),
  ('ENG-0152', 'English', 'Prepositions', 'Prepositions of time', 'hard', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.8', 'Choose the option that correctly fills the blank:
I will submit the report ______ Friday.', '["until", "since", "by", "during"]', 2, '''By'' means ''not later than'', which fits a deadline for a single action.', '{"0": "''Until'' is for an action that continues up to a time.", "1": "''Since'' needs a perfect tense and a starting point.", "3": "''During'' needs a period, not a day as a deadline."}'),
  ('ENG-0153', 'English', 'Prepositions', 'Prepositions of time', 'moderate', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.8', 'Choose the option that correctly fills the blank:
The OPD is open ______ 8 a.m. ______ 2 p.m.', '["since ... to", "between ... to", "at ... from", "from ... to"]', 3, 'A period with a start and end is ''from ... to'' (or ''between ... and'').', '{"0": "''Since'' cannot be paired with ''to''.", "1": "''Between'' must be paired with ''and''.", "2": "This reverses the logic and does not show a range."}'),
  ('ENG-0154', 'English', 'Prepositions', 'Prepositions of movement', 'moderate', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.8', 'Choose the option that correctly fills the blank:
The cat jumped ______ the table and ran out of the room.', '["off", "of", "in", "at"]', 0, '''Off'' shows movement away from a surface.', '{"1": "''Of'' does not show movement.", "2": "''In'' shows being inside.", "3": "''At'' shows a point, not leaving a surface."}'),
  ('ENG-0155', 'English', 'Prepositions', 'Prepositions of position', 'easy', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.8', 'Choose the option that correctly fills the blank:
The plane flew ______ the clouds, so the passengers could see clear blue sky.', '["below", "above", "among", "beneath"]', 1, 'If the passengers see clear blue sky, the plane is higher than the clouds: above.', '{"0": "Below the clouds, the sky would be hidden.", "2": "Among the clouds, the view would be cloudy.", "3": "Beneath means below."}'),
  ('ENG-0156', 'English', 'Prepositions', 'Prepositions of movement', 'moderate', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.8', 'Choose the option that correctly fills the blank:
He walked ______ the bank of the river for an hour.', '["across", "into", "along", "among"]', 2, '''Along'' means following the length of something.', '{"0": "''Across'' means from one side to the other.", "1": "''Into'' means entering.", "3": "''Among'' is for groups."}'),
  ('ENG-0157', 'English', 'Prepositions', 'Prepositions of position', 'moderate', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.8', 'Choose the option that correctly fills the blank:
The hospital is ______ the end of this road.', '["in", "on", "for", "at"]', 3, '''At the end of'' is the fixed expression for the point where something finishes.', '{"0": "''In the end'' means finally, not a place.", "1": "''On the end'' is not the standard phrase here.", "2": "''For'' does not show position."}'),
  ('ENG-0158', 'English', 'Prepositions', 'Fixed prepositions', 'easy', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.8', 'Choose the option that correctly fills the blank:
She is good ______ mathematics.', '["at", "in", "on", "with"]', 0, 'The adjective ''good'' (meaning skilled) takes ''at''.', '{"1": "''Good in'' is not the standard pairing for skill.", "2": "''Good on'' is not used for skill.", "3": "''Good with'' is used for people or tools, not subjects."}'),
  ('ENG-0159', 'English', 'Prepositions', 'Fixed prepositions', 'moderate', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.8', 'Choose the option that correctly fills the blank:
He insisted ______ paying the bill.', '["for", "on", "to", "at"]', 1, '''Insist'' takes the preposition ''on''.', '{"0": "''Insist for'' is incorrect.", "2": "''Insist to'' is incorrect.", "3": "''Insist at'' is incorrect."}'),
  ('ENG-0160', 'English', 'Prepositions', 'Fixed prepositions', 'moderate', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.8', 'Choose the option that correctly fills the blank:
She prefers tea ______ coffee.', '["than", "from", "to", "over than"]', 2, '''Prefer A to B'' is the standard pattern.', '{"0": "''Prefer ... than'' is a common error.", "1": "''From'' is not used with prefer.", "3": "''Over than'' is not English."}'),
  ('ENG-0161', 'English', 'Prepositions', 'Fixed prepositions', 'moderate', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.8', 'Choose the option that correctly fills the blank:
The suspect was accused ______ theft.', '["for", "with", "about", "of"]', 3, '''Accuse someone of something'' is the fixed pattern.', '{"0": "''Accused for'' is incorrect.", "1": "''Accused with'' is incorrect (we say ''charged with'').", "2": "''Accused about'' is incorrect."}'),
  ('ENG-0162', 'English', 'Prepositions', 'Fixed prepositions', 'easy', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.8', 'Choose the option that correctly fills the blank:
The patient is suffering ______ a high fever.', '["from", "with", "of", "by"]', 0, '''Suffer from'' is used for an illness or condition.', '{"1": "''Suffer with'' is not the standard form in tests.", "2": "''Suffer of'' is incorrect.", "3": "''By'' is not used with suffer."}'),
  ('ENG-0163', 'English', 'Transitional devices', 'Contrast', 'easy', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.9', 'Choose the option that correctly fills the blank:
He studied hard for months; ______, he failed the test.', '["therefore", "however", "moreover", "for example"]', 1, 'The second idea contrasts with the first (hard work, yet failure), so a contrast transition is needed.', '{"0": "''Therefore'' shows a result, but failing is not a result of studying hard.", "2": "''Moreover'' adds a similar point.", "3": "''For example'' introduces an example."}'),
  ('ENG-0164', 'English', 'Transitional devices', 'Cause and result', 'easy', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.9', 'Choose the option that correctly fills the blank:
It rained heavily all morning; ______, the match was cancelled.', '["however", "nevertheless", "consequently", "for instance"]', 2, 'The cancellation is a result of the rain, so a result transition is needed.', '{"0": "''However'' signals contrast.", "1": "''Nevertheless'' signals contrast.", "3": "''For instance'' introduces an example."}'),
  ('ENG-0165', 'English', 'Transitional devices', 'Addition', 'easy', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.9', 'Choose the option that correctly fills the blank:
Regular exercise strengthens the heart. ______, it reduces stress.', '["However", "On the contrary", "Otherwise", "Moreover"]', 3, 'The second sentence adds another benefit, so an addition transition is needed.', '{"0": "''However'' signals contrast.", "1": "''On the contrary'' rejects the previous statement.", "2": "''Otherwise'' shows a condition."}'),
  ('ENG-0166', 'English', 'Transitional devices', 'Example', 'easy', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.9', 'Choose the option that correctly fills the blank:
Many vegetables are rich in iron; spinach, ______, is an excellent source.', '["for instance", "however", "therefore", "otherwise"]', 0, 'Spinach is an example of an iron-rich vegetable.', '{"1": "There is no contrast.", "2": "Spinach is not a result of the first statement.", "3": "There is no condition."}'),
  ('ENG-0167', 'English', 'Transitional devices', 'Contrast', 'moderate', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.9', 'Choose the option that correctly fills the blank:
______ he was exhausted, he kept working until the last patient was seen.', '["Because", "Although", "Despite", "Unless"]', 1, '''Although'' introduces a clause that contrasts with the main clause.', '{"0": "Being exhausted is not a reason to keep working.", "2": "''Despite'' must be followed by a noun or -ing form, not a full clause.", "3": "''Unless'' means ''if not''."}'),
  ('ENG-0168', 'English', 'Transitional devices', 'Contrast', 'moderate', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.9', 'Choose the option that correctly fills the blank:
______ the heavy traffic, we reached the hospital on time.', '["Although", "Even though", "Despite", "Because"]', 2, '''Despite'' is followed by a noun phrase (''the heavy traffic'') and shows contrast.', '{"0": "''Although'' must be followed by a clause with a verb.", "1": "''Even though'' must be followed by a clause with a verb.", "3": "Traffic is not a reason for arriving on time."}'),
  ('ENG-0169', 'English', 'Transitional devices', 'Condition', 'moderate', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.9', 'Choose the option that correctly fills the blank:
Take an umbrella; ______, you will get soaked.', '["moreover", "similarly", "consequently", "otherwise"]', 3, '''Otherwise'' means ''if not'', which fits the warning.', '{"0": "''Moreover'' adds a point.", "1": "''Similarly'' compares.", "2": "Getting soaked is not a result of taking an umbrella."}')
) AS s
JOIN users u ON u.email = 'hozaiphaa@gmail.com';

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions
  (id, subject, chapter, topic, difficulty, source, stem, options, answer, why, wrong, stage, free, checks, writer_id, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT s.column1, s.column2, s.column3, s.column4, s.column5, s.column6, s.column7, s.column8, s.column9, s.column10, s.column11,
  'live', 0, '[]', u.id, unixepoch() * 1000, unixepoch() * 1000
FROM (VALUES
  ('ENG-0170', 'English', 'Transitional devices', 'Sequence', 'easy', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.9', 'Choose the best word for the blank:
First, wash the wound with clean water. ______, apply an antiseptic. Finally, cover it with a dressing.', '["Next", "However", "In conclusion", "On the other hand"]', 0, 'The steps are in order, so a sequence word is needed between ''First'' and ''Finally''.', '{"1": "There is no contrast.", "2": "''In conclusion'' sums up; it does not mark a middle step.", "3": "There is no contrast."}'),
  ('ENG-0171', 'English', 'Transitional devices', 'Conclusion', 'easy', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.9', 'Choose the option that correctly fills the blank:
______, a balanced diet, regular exercise and enough sleep are the keys to good health.', '["For example", "In conclusion", "Nevertheless", "In contrast"]', 1, 'The sentence sums up the main points, so a concluding transition fits.', '{"0": "It is not an example of something before it.", "2": "There is no contrast.", "3": "There is no contrast."}'),
  ('ENG-0172', 'English', 'Transitional devices', 'Contrast', 'easy', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.9', 'Choose the option that correctly fills the blank:
Ali is very talkative; his brother, ______, hardly says a word.', '["similarly", "therefore", "on the other hand", "as a result"]', 2, 'The brothers are being contrasted.', '{"0": "''Similarly'' shows likeness.", "1": "The brother''s silence is not a result of Ali''s talking.", "3": "The brother''s silence is not a result of Ali''s talking."}'),
  ('ENG-0173', 'English', 'Transitional devices', 'Comparison', 'easy', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.9', 'Choose the option that correctly fills the blank:
Sana finds chemistry fascinating. ______, her twin sister loves the subject.', '["In contrast", "Otherwise", "Nevertheless", "Likewise"]', 3, 'Both sisters like chemistry, so a similarity transition is needed.', '{"0": "There is no contrast.", "1": "There is no condition.", "2": "There is no contrast."}'),
  ('ENG-0174', 'English', 'Transitional devices', 'Condition', 'moderate', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.9', 'Choose the option that correctly fills the blank:
You will pass ______ you revise regularly.', '["provided that", "unless", "although", "whereas"]', 0, '''Provided that'' means ''on condition that'', which fits passing because of regular revision.', '{"1": "''Unless'' means ''if not'', reversing the meaning.", "2": "''Although'' shows contrast.", "3": "''Whereas'' compares two facts."}'),
  ('ENG-0175', 'English', 'Transitional devices', 'Condition', 'moderate', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.9', 'Choose the option that correctly fills the blank:
______ you leave now, you will miss the flight.', '["If", "Unless", "Because", "Since"]', 1, '''Unless'' means ''if ... not'': if you do not leave now, you will miss it.', '{"0": "''If you leave now, you will miss the flight'' makes no sense.", "2": "Leaving now would not cause missing the flight.", "3": "Leaving now would not cause missing the flight."}'),
  ('ENG-0176', 'English', 'Transitional devices', 'Cause and result', 'easy', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.9', 'Choose the option that correctly fills the blank:
He missed the bus ______ he woke up late.', '["so", "although", "because", "unless"]', 2, 'Waking up late is the reason for missing the bus.', '{"0": "''So'' would make missing the bus the cause of waking late.", "1": "There is no contrast.", "3": "There is no condition."}'),
  ('ENG-0177', 'English', 'Transitional devices', 'Addition', 'moderate', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.9', 'Choose the option that correctly fills the blank:
Not only did he finish first, ______ he also broke the record.', '["and", "or", "yet", "but"]', 3, 'The correlative pair is ''not only ... but also''.', '{"0": "''Not only ... and also'' is incorrect.", "1": "''Or'' offers a choice.", "2": "''Not only ... yet also'' is incorrect."}'),
  ('ENG-0178', 'English', 'Transitional devices', 'Contrast', 'easy', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.9', 'Which of these transitions shows CONTRAST?', '["nevertheless", "furthermore", "consequently", "for instance"]', 0, '''Nevertheless'' (in spite of that) introduces a contrasting idea.', '{"1": "''Furthermore'' adds information.", "2": "''Consequently'' shows a result.", "3": "''For instance'' gives an example."}'),
  ('ENG-0179', 'English', 'Punctuation', 'Apostrophe', 'easy', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.10', 'Which sentence is punctuated correctly?', '["Its a beautiful day.", "It''s a beautiful day.", "Its'' a beautiful day.", "It,s a beautiful day."]', 1, '''It''s'' is the contraction of ''it is'', and the apostrophe replaces the missing ''i''.', '{"0": "''Its'' is a possessive, not ''it is''.", "2": "''Its'''' is not a word.", "3": "A comma cannot replace an apostrophe."}'),
  ('ENG-0180', 'English', 'Punctuation', 'Apostrophe', 'moderate', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.10', 'Which sentence is punctuated correctly?', '["The two doctor''s reports were identical.", "The two doctors reports'' were identical.", "The two doctors'' reports were identical.", "The two doctors''s reports were identical."]', 2, 'For a plural noun ending in -s, the possessive apostrophe goes after the s: doctors''.', '{"0": "''Doctor''s'' is the singular possessive, but there are two doctors.", "1": "The apostrophe belongs on the owner (doctors), not the thing owned.", "3": "A plural ending in -s takes only an apostrophe, not ''s."}'),
  ('ENG-0181', 'English', 'Punctuation', 'Apostrophe', 'moderate', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.10', 'Which sentence is punctuated correctly?', '["The childrens'' ward is on the second floor.", "The childrens ward is on the second floor.", "The children'' ward is on the second floor.", "The children''s ward is on the second floor."]', 3, '''Children'' is a plural that does not end in -s, so it takes ''s: children''s.', '{"0": "''Childrens'' is not a word.", "1": "A possessive apostrophe is missing.", "2": "A plural not ending in -s needs ''s."}'),
  ('ENG-0182', 'English', 'Punctuation', 'Comma', 'easy', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.10', 'Which sentence is punctuated correctly?', '["However, the results were not significant.", "However the results, were not significant.", "However the results were, not significant.", "However; the results were not significant."]', 0, 'A transition word at the start of a sentence is followed by a comma.', '{"1": "A comma cannot separate the subject from its verb.", "2": "A comma cannot separate ''were'' from its complement.", "3": "A semicolon is not used after an introductory word."}'),
  ('ENG-0183', 'English', 'Punctuation', 'Question mark', 'moderate', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.10', 'Which sentence is punctuated correctly?', '["She asked me where I lived?", "She asked me where I lived.", "She asked me, where I lived?", "She asked me where did I live."]', 1, 'An indirect (reported) question is a statement, so it ends with a full stop and keeps normal word order.', '{"0": "An indirect question does not take a question mark.", "2": "No comma or question mark is needed in an indirect question.", "3": "Reported questions do not use question word order (did I live)."}'),
  ('ENG-0184', 'English', 'Punctuation', 'Colon', 'moderate', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.10', 'Which sentence is punctuated correctly?', '["You will need three things; a pen, a ruler and a calculator.", "You will need: three things a pen, a ruler and a calculator.", "You will need three things: a pen, a ruler and a calculator.", "You will need three things, a pen; a ruler; and a calculator."]', 2, 'A colon introduces a list after a complete clause.', '{"0": "A semicolon does not introduce a list.", "1": "The colon should come after the complete clause ''three things''.", "3": "Semicolons are not needed between simple list items."}'),
  ('ENG-0185', 'English', 'Punctuation', 'Semicolon', 'hard', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.10', 'Which sentence is punctuated correctly?', '["I wanted to go, however, it was raining.", "I wanted to go however; it was raining.", "I wanted to go; however it, was raining.", "I wanted to go; however, it was raining."]', 3, 'Two independent clauses joined by ''however'' need a semicolon before it and a comma after it.', '{"0": "A comma alone cannot join two independent clauses (comma splice).", "1": "The semicolon must come before ''however'', not after.", "2": "The comma after ''it'' splits the subject from its verb."}'),
  ('ENG-0186', 'English', 'Punctuation', 'Quotation marks', 'moderate', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.10', 'Which sentence is punctuated correctly?', '["\"Close the door,\" said the teacher.", "\"Close the door\" said the teacher.", "\"Close the door.\" said the teacher.", "\"Close the door\", said the teacher,"]', 0, 'When a quotation comes before the reporting clause, a comma ends the quotation inside the quotation marks.', '{"1": "A comma is needed to end the quoted words.", "2": "A full stop cannot end a quotation followed by ''said the teacher''.", "3": "The comma should be inside the closing quotation mark, and the sentence must end with a full stop."}'),
  ('ENG-0187', 'English', 'Punctuation', 'Exclamation mark', 'easy', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.10', 'Which sentence is punctuated correctly?', '["What a brilliant idea?", "What a brilliant idea!", "What a brilliant idea.", "What a brilliant idea,"]', 1, 'An exclamatory sentence beginning with ''What a ...'' ends with an exclamation mark.', '{"0": "It is an exclamation, not a question.", "2": "A strong feeling is shown, so an exclamation mark is needed.", "3": "A sentence cannot end with a comma."}'),
  ('ENG-0188', 'English', 'Punctuation', 'Comma', 'easy', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.10', 'Which sentence is punctuated correctly?', '["When the bell rang the students, left the hall.", "When, the bell rang the students left the hall.", "When the bell rang, the students left the hall.", "When the bell rang; the students left the hall."]', 2, 'An introductory adverb clause is followed by a comma.', '{"0": "A comma cannot separate the subject from its verb.", "1": "A comma cannot come after ''When''.", "3": "A semicolon cannot join a dependent clause to a main clause."}'),
  ('ENG-0189', 'English', 'Punctuation', 'Comma', 'hard', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.10', 'Which sentence is punctuated correctly?', '["My mother who is a doctor works at a district hospital.", "My mother, who is a doctor works at a district hospital.", "My mother who is a doctor, works at a district hospital.", "My mother, who is a doctor, works at a district hospital."]', 3, 'A person has only one mother, so ''who is a doctor'' is extra (non-defining) information and is set off by a pair of commas.', '{"0": "Without commas the clause looks defining, as if the speaker had several mothers.", "1": "The closing comma after ''doctor'' is missing.", "2": "The opening comma after ''mother'' is missing."}')
) AS s
JOIN users u ON u.email = 'hozaiphaa@gmail.com';

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions
  (id, subject, chapter, topic, difficulty, source, stem, options, answer, why, wrong, stage, free, checks, writer_id, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT s.column1, s.column2, s.column3, s.column4, s.column5, s.column6, s.column7, s.column8, s.column9, s.column10, s.column11,
  'live', 0, '[]', u.id, unixepoch() * 1000, unixepoch() * 1000
FROM (VALUES
  ('ENG-0190', 'English', 'Punctuation', 'Capital letters', 'easy', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.10', 'Which sentence is punctuated correctly?', '["We visited the Badshahi Mosque in Lahore last Friday.", "We visited the badshahi mosque in lahore last friday.", "We visited the Badshahi mosque in Lahore last friday.", "we visited the Badshahi Mosque in Lahore last Friday."]', 0, 'Names of buildings, cities and days of the week take capital letters, as does the first word of a sentence.', '{"1": "Proper nouns need capitals.", "2": "''Mosque'' is part of the name and ''Friday'' is a day; both need capitals.", "3": "The first word of a sentence takes a capital."}'),
  ('ENG-0191', 'English', 'Punctuation', 'Apostrophe', 'easy', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.10', 'Which punctuation mark shows that letters have been left out, as in "don''t"?', '["Comma", "Apostrophe", "Hyphen", "Colon"]', 1, 'In a contraction, the apostrophe stands for the missing letters (do not → don''t).', '{"0": "A comma separates parts of a sentence.", "2": "A hyphen joins words.", "3": "A colon introduces lists or explanations."}'),
  ('ENG-0192', 'English', 'Punctuation', 'Hyphen', 'moderate', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.10', 'Which sentence is punctuated correctly?', '["She is a well known-author.", "She is a well, known author.", "She is a well-known author.", "She is a well-known-author."]', 2, 'A compound adjective before a noun is hyphenated: well-known author.', '{"0": "The hyphen goes between ''well'' and ''known'', not before ''author''.", "1": "A comma cannot split a compound adjective.", "3": "The noun ''author'' is not part of the compound."}'),
  ('ENG-0193', 'English', 'Punctuation', 'Capital letters', 'moderate', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.10', 'Which sentence is punctuated and capitalised correctly?', '["Dr. Ayesha works at the WHO office in Islamabad.", "dr. Ayesha works at the who office in islamabad.", "Dr. ayesha works at the Who office in Islamabad.", "Dr Ayesha works at the W.h.o office in islamabad."]', 0, 'Titles, names, cities and abbreviations of organisations (WHO) are capitalised.', '{"1": "The title, the abbreviation and the city need capitals.", "2": "A personal name and an abbreviation need capitals.", "3": "The abbreviation is mis-capitalised and the city needs a capital."}'),
  ('ENG-0194', 'English', 'Phrases, clauses and inversion', 'Phrases and clauses', 'moderate', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.11', 'In "The boy with a red cap is my cousin.", the words "with a red cap" form:', '["an adverb phrase", "a noun clause", "an adverb clause", "an adjective phrase"]', 3, 'The phrase has no subject-verb pair and describes the noun ''boy'', so it is an adjective phrase.', '{"0": "It describes a noun, not a verb.", "1": "It has no finite verb, so it is not a clause.", "2": "It has no finite verb, so it is not a clause."}'),
  ('ENG-0195', 'English', 'Phrases, clauses and inversion', 'Phrases and clauses', 'moderate', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.11', 'In "She left the hostel before sunrise.", the words "before sunrise" form:', '["an adverb phrase", "an adjective phrase", "a noun clause", "an adjective clause"]', 0, 'The phrase tells when she left, modifying the verb, and has no finite verb.', '{"1": "It does not describe a noun.", "2": "It has no finite verb.", "3": "It has no finite verb."}'),
  ('ENG-0196', 'English', 'Phrases, clauses and inversion', 'Phrases and clauses', 'moderate', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.11', 'In "I know that he is honest.", the words "that he is honest" form:', '["an adjective clause", "a noun clause", "an adverb clause", "a phrase"]', 1, 'It has its own subject and verb (he is) and acts as the object of ''know'', so it is a noun clause.', '{"0": "It does not describe a noun.", "2": "It does not tell how, when or why.", "3": "It contains a subject and finite verb, so it is a clause."}'),
  ('ENG-0197', 'English', 'Phrases, clauses and inversion', 'Phrases and clauses', 'moderate', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.11', 'In "The man who helped us is a doctor.", the words "who helped us" form:', '["a noun clause", "an adverb clause", "an adjective clause", "an adverb phrase"]', 2, 'A relative clause beginning with ''who'' describes the noun ''man'', so it is an adjective clause.', '{"0": "It is not used as a subject or object.", "1": "It does not modify a verb.", "3": "It has a finite verb, so it is a clause."}'),
  ('ENG-0198', 'English', 'Phrases, clauses and inversion', 'Phrases and clauses', 'moderate', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.11', 'In "We stayed indoors because it was raining.", the words "because it was raining" form:', '["an adjective clause", "a noun clause", "a prepositional phrase", "an adverb clause of reason"]', 3, 'The clause tells why we stayed indoors, so it is an adverb clause of reason.', '{"0": "It does not describe a noun.", "1": "It is not the subject or object.", "2": "It has a subject and finite verb, so it is a clause."}'),
  ('ENG-0199', 'English', 'Phrases, clauses and inversion', 'Phrases and clauses', 'easy', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.11', 'Which of these best describes a clause?', '["A group of words with its own subject and finite verb", "A group of words with no subject or finite verb", "A single word that names a person", "A complete paragraph"]', 0, 'A clause has a subject and a finite verb; a phrase does not.', '{"1": "This describes a phrase.", "2": "This describes a noun.", "3": "A paragraph is made of several sentences."}'),
  ('ENG-0200', 'English', 'Phrases, clauses and inversion', 'Kinds of sentences', 'moderate', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.11', '"I called him twice, but he did not answer." This sentence is:', '["simple", "compound", "complex", "compound-complex"]', 1, 'Two independent clauses joined by the coordinating conjunction ''but'' make a compound sentence.', '{"0": "A simple sentence has one clause.", "2": "A complex sentence needs a dependent clause.", "3": "There is no dependent clause."}'),
  ('ENG-0201', 'English', 'Phrases, clauses and inversion', 'Kinds of sentences', 'moderate', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.11', '"Although he was ill, he attended the class." This sentence is:', '["simple", "compound", "complex", "compound-complex"]', 2, 'One independent clause plus one dependent clause (Although he was ill) makes a complex sentence.', '{"0": "A simple sentence has one clause.", "1": "A compound sentence joins two independent clauses.", "3": "That would need two independent clauses plus a dependent one."}'),
  ('ENG-0202', 'English', 'Phrases, clauses and inversion', 'Kinds of sentences', 'moderate', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.11', 'Which of these is a SIMPLE sentence?', '["The doctor was tired, so he went home.", "The doctor went home because he was tired.", "When his shift ended, the doctor went home.", "The tired doctor went home after the night shift."]', 3, 'It has one subject-verb pair (doctor went); the other words are phrases.', '{"0": "Two independent clauses joined by ''so'' make it compound.", "1": "''Because he was tired'' is a dependent clause, so it is complex.", "2": "''When his shift ended'' is a dependent clause, so it is complex."}'),
  ('ENG-0203', 'English', 'Phrases, clauses and inversion', 'Kinds of sentences', 'easy', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.11', '"Switch off the lights before you leave." By function, this sentence is:', '["imperative", "declarative", "interrogative", "exclamatory"]', 0, 'It gives a command or instruction, so it is imperative.', '{"1": "A declarative sentence makes a statement.", "2": "An interrogative sentence asks a question.", "3": "An exclamatory sentence expresses strong feeling."}'),
  ('ENG-0204', 'English', 'Phrases, clauses and inversion', 'Misplaced modifiers', 'hard', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.11', 'Which sentence places the modifying phrase correctly?', '["Walking to college, an accident was seen by me.", "Walking to college, I saw an accident.", "Walking to college, the accident happened.", "An accident, walking to college, I saw."]', 1, 'An opening -ing phrase must describe the subject that follows it; ''I'' was the one walking.', '{"0": "This suggests the accident was walking (dangling modifier).", "2": "This suggests the accident was walking.", "3": "The phrase is misplaced and breaks the sentence."}'),
  ('ENG-0205', 'English', 'Phrases, clauses and inversion', 'Inversion', 'moderate', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.12', 'Choose the option that correctly fills the blank:
Never ______ such a beautiful sunset.', '["I have seen", "I saw", "have I seen", "seen I have"]', 2, 'When a negative adverb like ''never'' opens a sentence, the auxiliary comes before the subject.', '{"0": "Normal word order is not used after a fronted ''Never''.", "1": "Normal word order is not used after a fronted ''Never''.", "3": "The auxiliary must come before the subject, and the participle after."}'),
  ('ENG-0206', 'English', 'Phrases, clauses and inversion', 'Inversion', 'hard', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.12', 'Choose the option that correctly fills the blank:
Hardly ______ the hospital when it started to rain.', '["we had left", "we left", "did we left", "had we left"]', 3, '''Hardly'' at the start takes inversion with the past perfect, and is followed by ''when''.', '{"0": "Normal word order is not used after a fronted ''Hardly''.", "1": "Inversion with an auxiliary is needed.", "2": "''Did'' must be followed by the base form, and this pattern uses ''had''."}'),
  ('ENG-0207', 'English', 'Phrases, clauses and inversion', 'Inversion', 'moderate', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.12', 'Choose the option that correctly fills the blank:
No sooner had the teacher entered ______ the students stood up.', '["than", "when", "then", "that"]', 0, 'The fixed pair is ''No sooner ... than''.', '{"1": "''When'' goes with ''Hardly'' or ''Scarcely'', not ''No sooner''.", "2": "''Then'' is an adverb of time, not part of this pair.", "3": "''That'' does not complete this structure."}'),
  ('ENG-0208', 'English', 'Phrases, clauses and inversion', 'Inversion', 'hard', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.12', 'Choose the option that correctly fills the blank:
Not only ______ the exam, but she also topped the province.', '["she passed", "did she pass", "she did pass", "passed she"]', 1, 'After ''Not only'' at the start of a clause, the auxiliary comes before the subject.', '{"0": "Inversion is needed after a fronted ''Not only''.", "2": "The auxiliary must come before the subject.", "3": "Main verbs are not inverted in modern English; ''did'' is used."}'),
  ('ENG-0209', 'English', 'Phrases, clauses and inversion', 'Inversion', 'moderate', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.12', 'Choose the option that correctly fills the blank:
Rarely ______ such dedication in a first-year student.', '["we see", "we do see", "do we see", "see we"]', 2, 'A fronted negative adverb such as ''rarely'' takes inversion with do/does/did.', '{"0": "Inversion is needed after ''Rarely''.", "1": "The auxiliary must come before the subject.", "3": "Main verbs are not inverted; ''do'' is used."}')
) AS s
JOIN users u ON u.email = 'hozaiphaa@gmail.com';

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions
  (id, subject, chapter, topic, difficulty, source, stem, options, answer, why, wrong, stage, free, checks, writer_id, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT s.column1, s.column2, s.column3, s.column4, s.column5, s.column6, s.column7, s.column8, s.column9, s.column10, s.column11,
  'live', 0, '[]', u.id, unixepoch() * 1000, unixepoch() * 1000
FROM (VALUES
  ('ENG-0210', 'English', 'Phrases, clauses and inversion', 'Inversion', 'hard', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.12', 'Choose the option that correctly fills the blank:
Only after the results were announced ______ how hard the paper had been.', '["they realised", "they did realise", "realised they", "did they realise"]', 3, '''Only after ...'' at the start of a sentence takes inversion in the main clause.', '{"0": "Inversion is needed after ''Only after ...''.", "1": "The auxiliary must come before the subject.", "2": "Main verbs are not inverted; ''did'' is used."}'),
  ('ENG-0211', 'English', 'Phrases, clauses and inversion', 'Inversion', 'moderate', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.12', '"Had I known about the test, I would have prepared." This sentence means:', '["If I had known about the test, I would have prepared.", "When I knew about the test, I prepared.", "Because I knew about the test, I prepared.", "If I know about the test, I will prepare."]', 0, 'Inverting ''had'' and the subject is a formal way of writing an if-clause in the third conditional.', '{"1": "The original says I did NOT know and did NOT prepare.", "2": "The original says I did NOT know.", "3": "The original is about the past, not a future possibility."}'),
  ('ENG-0212', 'English', 'Phrases, clauses and inversion', 'Inversion', 'moderate', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.12', '"Should you need any help, call the ward nurse." This sentence means:', '["You should need help before calling the nurse.", "If you need any help, call the ward nurse.", "You must call the nurse whether or not you need help.", "Because you need help, the nurse will call you."]', 1, 'Inverted ''should'' at the start is a formal way of saying ''if''.', '{"0": "''Should'' here is not about obligation.", "2": "The call depends on needing help.", "3": "The roles are reversed: you call the nurse."}'),
  ('ENG-0213', 'English', 'Phrases, clauses and inversion', 'Sentence fragments', 'moderate', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.11', 'Which of these is a sentence FRAGMENT (not a complete sentence)?', '["The lab was closed on Sunday.", "We could not work because the lab was closed.", "Because the lab was closed on Sunday.", "Close the lab on Sunday."]', 2, 'A clause beginning with ''because'' is dependent; on its own it does not express a complete thought.', '{"0": "This has a subject and verb and is complete.", "1": "The dependent clause is attached to a main clause.", "3": "An imperative with an understood subject (you) is complete."}'),
  ('ENG-0214', 'English', 'Phrases, clauses and inversion', 'Sentence fragments', 'moderate', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.11', 'Which option correctly fixes the fragment?
"After the results were announced. The students celebrated."', '["After the results were announced; the students celebrated.", "After the results were announced. And the students celebrated.", "After, the results were announced the students celebrated.", "After the results were announced, the students celebrated."]', 3, 'Joining the dependent clause to the main clause with a comma makes one complete sentence.', '{"0": "A semicolon cannot join a dependent clause to a main clause.", "1": "This still leaves the first part as a fragment.", "2": "The comma after ''After'' breaks the clause."}'),
  ('ENG-0215', 'English', 'Active and passive voice', 'Active to passive', 'easy', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.13', 'Choose the correct passive form of:
"The nurse cleaned the wound."', '["The wound is cleaned by the nurse.", "The wound was cleaned by the nurse.", "The wound has been cleaned by the nurse.", "The wound was being cleaned by the nurse."]', 1, 'Simple past active becomes was/were + past participle in the passive.', '{"0": "''Is cleaned'' is simple present, not past.", "2": "''Has been cleaned'' is present perfect.", "3": "''Was being cleaned'' is past continuous."}'),
  ('ENG-0216', 'English', 'Active and passive voice', 'Active to passive', 'moderate', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.13', 'Choose the correct passive form of:
"They are building a new hospital."', '["A new hospital is built.", "A new hospital was being built.", "A new hospital is being built.", "A new hospital has been built."]', 2, 'Present continuous active becomes is/are + being + past participle.', '{"0": "''Is built'' drops the continuous sense.", "1": "''Was being built'' is past, not present.", "3": "''Has been built'' is present perfect."}'),
  ('ENG-0217', 'English', 'Active and passive voice', 'Active to passive', 'moderate', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.13', 'Choose the correct passive form of:
"She has written three books."', '["Three books has been written by her.", "Three books were written by her.", "Three books had been written by her.", "Three books have been written by her."]', 3, 'Present perfect active becomes has/have + been + past participle; ''books'' is plural, so ''have''.', '{"0": "''Books'' is plural and needs ''have''.", "1": "''Were written'' is simple past, not present perfect.", "2": "''Had been written'' is past perfect."}'),
  ('ENG-0218', 'English', 'Active and passive voice', 'Questions in the passive', 'hard', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.13', 'Choose the correct passive form of:
"Who broke the window?"', '["By whom was the window broken?", "Who was broken by the window?", "By whom the window was broken?", "Whom did the window break?"]', 0, 'A ''who'' question in the simple past becomes ''By whom + was/were + subject + past participle?''.', '{"1": "This changes the meaning completely.", "2": "A question needs the auxiliary ''was'' before the subject.", "3": "This is not passive and changes the meaning."}'),
  ('ENG-0219', 'English', 'Active and passive voice', 'Imperatives in the passive', 'moderate', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.13', 'Choose the correct passive form of:
"Open the door."', '["The door is opened.", "Let the door be opened.", "The door was opened.", "The door has been opened."]', 1, 'An imperative becomes passive with ''Let + object + be + past participle''.', '{"0": "This is a statement, not a command.", "2": "This is a past statement.", "3": "This is a present perfect statement."}'),
  ('ENG-0220', 'English', 'Active and passive voice', 'Active to passive', 'easy', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.13', 'Choose the correct passive form of:
"The police will arrest the thief."', '["The thief would be arrested by the police.", "The thief will arrest by the police.", "The thief will be arrested by the police.", "The thief will have been arrested by the police."]', 2, 'Future simple active becomes will + be + past participle.', '{"0": "''Would'' changes the tense.", "1": "The passive needs ''be'' + past participle.", "3": "That is future perfect."}'),
  ('ENG-0221', 'English', 'Active and passive voice', 'Active to passive', 'moderate', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.13', 'Choose the correct passive form of:
"The doctor had examined the patient."', '["The patient has been examined by the doctor.", "The patient was examined by the doctor.", "The patient had examined the doctor.", "The patient had been examined by the doctor."]', 3, 'Past perfect active becomes had + been + past participle.', '{"0": "''Has been'' is present perfect.", "1": "''Was examined'' is simple past.", "2": "This is still active and reverses who examined whom."}'),
  ('ENG-0222', 'English', 'Active and passive voice', 'Active to passive', 'easy', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.13', 'Choose the correct passive form of:
"People speak English all over the world."', '["English is spoken all over the world.", "English was spoken all over the world.", "English is speaking all over the world.", "English has spoken all over the world."]', 0, 'Simple present active becomes is/are + past participle; ''by people'' is usually dropped because the doer is general.', '{"1": "''Was spoken'' changes the tense to past.", "2": "''Is speaking'' is active continuous.", "3": "''Has spoken'' is active present perfect."}'),
  ('ENG-0223', 'English', 'Active and passive voice', 'Questions in the passive', 'moderate', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.13', 'Choose the correct passive form of:
"Did he complete the task?"', '["Did the task complete by him?", "Was the task completed by him?", "Has the task been completed by him?", "Was the task being completed by him?"]', 1, 'A simple past question becomes ''Was/Were + subject + past participle?''.', '{"0": "The passive needs ''was'' + past participle, not ''did''.", "2": "''Has been completed'' changes the tense.", "3": "''Was being completed'' is past continuous."}'),
  ('ENG-0224', 'English', 'Active and passive voice', 'Modals in the passive', 'moderate', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.13', 'Choose the correct passive form of:
"We must obey the rules."', '["The rules must obey.", "The rules must have obeyed.", "The rules must be obeyed.", "The rules must been obeyed."]', 2, 'Modal + verb becomes modal + be + past participle.', '{"0": "This makes the rules the doers.", "1": "''Must have obeyed'' is a different structure and still active.", "3": "''Be'' is needed after the modal, not ''been''."}'),
  ('ENG-0225', 'English', 'Active and passive voice', 'Active to passive', 'moderate', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.13', 'Choose the correct passive form of:
"The cat was chasing the mouse."', '["The mouse was chased by the cat.", "The mouse is being chased by the cat.", "The mouse had been chased by the cat.", "The mouse was being chased by the cat."]', 3, 'Past continuous active becomes was/were + being + past participle.', '{"0": "This drops the continuous sense.", "1": "''Is being'' is present, not past.", "2": "''Had been chased'' is past perfect."}'),
  ('ENG-0226', 'English', 'Active and passive voice', 'Active to passive', 'easy', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.13', 'Choose the correct passive form of:
"Somebody has stolen my bag."', '["My bag has been stolen.", "My bag had been stolen.", "My bag was being stolen.", "My bag has stolen."]', 0, 'Present perfect active becomes has/have + been + past participle; ''by somebody'' is dropped because the doer is unknown.', '{"1": "''Had been stolen'' is past perfect.", "2": "''Was being stolen'' is past continuous.", "3": "''Has stolen'' is still active."}'),
  ('ENG-0227', 'English', 'Active and passive voice', 'Questions in the passive', 'hard', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.13', 'Choose the correct passive form of:
"Why did you scold him?"', '["Why did he scold by you?", "Why was he scolded by you?", "Why he was scolded by you?", "Why was he scolding by you?"]', 1, 'A ''why'' question in the simple past becomes ''Why + was/were + subject + past participle?''.', '{"0": "The passive needs ''was'' + past participle.", "2": "A question needs ''was'' before the subject.", "3": "''Scolding'' is not a past participle."}'),
  ('ENG-0228', 'English', 'Active and passive voice', 'Passive to active', 'easy', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.13', 'Choose the correct active form of:
"The match was won by Pakistan."', '["Pakistan has won the match.", "Pakistan wins the match.", "Pakistan won the match.", "Pakistan was winning the match."]', 2, 'Simple past passive (was won) becomes simple past active (won).', '{"0": "Present perfect changes the tense.", "1": "Simple present changes the tense.", "3": "Past continuous changes the tense."}'),
  ('ENG-0229', 'English', 'Active and passive voice', 'Recognising voice', 'easy', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.13', 'Which sentence is in the passive voice?', '["The board announced the results on Monday.", "The students checked their results on Monday.", "The results arrived on Monday.", "The results were announced on Monday."]', 3, '''Were announced'' is be + past participle, and the subject receives the action.', '{"0": "The subject (board) does the action, so it is active.", "1": "The subject (students) does the action, so it is active.", "2": "''Arrived'' is an active intransitive verb."}')
) AS s
JOIN users u ON u.email = 'hozaiphaa@gmail.com';

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions
  (id, subject, chapter, topic, difficulty, source, stem, options, answer, why, wrong, stage, free, checks, writer_id, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT s.column1, s.column2, s.column3, s.column4, s.column5, s.column6, s.column7, s.column8, s.column9, s.column10, s.column11,
  'live', 0, '[]', u.id, unixepoch() * 1000, unixepoch() * 1000
FROM (VALUES
  ('ENG-0230', 'English', 'Active and passive voice', 'Recognising voice', 'hard', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.13', 'Which sentence CANNOT be changed into the passive voice?', '["The baby slept peacefully.", "The baby drank the milk.", "The mother fed the baby.", "The doctor examined the baby."]', 0, 'Only transitive verbs (verbs with an object) have a passive. ''Slept'' has no object.', '{"1": "''The milk was drunk by the baby'' is possible.", "2": "''The baby was fed by the mother'' is possible.", "3": "''The baby was examined by the doctor'' is possible."}'),
  ('ENG-0231', 'English', 'Active and passive voice', 'Using the passive', 'moderate', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.13', 'In a laboratory report, a student writes "The solution was heated to 60 °C." Why is the passive voice a good choice here?', '["The doer is more important than the action", "The action matters more than who did it", "The passive voice is always shorter", "The active voice cannot be used in science"]', 1, 'The passive puts the focus on the process and leaves out an unimportant or obvious doer.', '{"0": "It is the other way round: the passive hides the doer.", "2": "Passive sentences are often longer, not shorter.", "3": "Scientists can use the active voice; the passive is a choice."}'),
  ('ENG-0232', 'English', 'Direct and indirect speech', 'Statements', 'easy', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.14', 'Choose the correct indirect form of:
He said, "I am tired."', '["He said that he is tired.", "He said that I was tired.", "He said that he was tired.", "He said that he had tired."]', 2, 'After a past reporting verb, ''am'' shifts back to ''was'', and ''I'' becomes ''he''.', '{"0": "The tense should shift back from ''am'' to ''was''.", "1": "The pronoun should change from ''I'' to ''he''.", "3": "''Had tired'' changes the meaning."}'),
  ('ENG-0233', 'English', 'Direct and indirect speech', 'Statements', 'moderate', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.14', 'Choose the correct indirect form of:
She said, "I will come tomorrow."', '["She said that she will come tomorrow.", "She said that she would come tomorrow.", "She said that I would come the next day.", "She said that she would come the next day."]', 3, '''Will'' becomes ''would'' and ''tomorrow'' becomes ''the next day'' (or ''the following day'').', '{"0": "Both ''will'' and ''tomorrow'' should change.", "1": "''Tomorrow'' should change to ''the next day''.", "2": "The pronoun should change from ''I'' to ''she''."}'),
  ('ENG-0234', 'English', 'Direct and indirect speech', 'Statements', 'hard', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.14', 'Choose the correct indirect form of:
The teacher said, "The earth revolves around the sun."', '["The teacher said that the earth revolves around the sun.", "The teacher said that the earth had revolved around the sun.", "The teacher said that the earth would revolve around the sun.", "The teacher said that the earth is revolving around the sun."]', 0, 'A universal truth can keep the simple present in reported speech.', '{"1": "The past perfect suggests the earth no longer revolves, which is false.", "2": "''Would revolve'' changes the meaning to a future or condition.", "3": "The continuous form changes a permanent truth into a temporary action."}'),
  ('ENG-0235', 'English', 'Direct and indirect speech', 'Statements', 'moderate', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.14', 'Choose the correct indirect form of:
He said to me, "Where do you live?"', '["He asked me where did I live.", "He asked me where I lived.", "He said to me where I live.", "He asked me where do I live?"]', 1, '''Said to'' becomes ''asked'', the question becomes a statement order (I lived), and the tense shifts back.', '{"0": "Reported questions do not use ''did'' and question order.", "2": "A question is reported with ''asked'', and the tense should shift.", "3": "Reported questions do not use question order or a question mark."}'),
  ('ENG-0236', 'English', 'Direct and indirect speech', 'Commands and requests', 'moderate', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.14', 'Choose the correct indirect form of:
She said to him, "Please help me."', '["She told him that please help her.", "She requested him that he helps me.", "She requested him to help her.", "She said him to help me."]', 2, 'A polite request is reported with ''requested'' + object + to-infinitive, and ''me'' becomes ''her''.', '{"0": "''Please'' is dropped and a to-infinitive is used.", "1": "The pattern is ''requested him to help'', and ''me'' should become ''her''.", "3": "''Said him'' is incorrect, and ''me'' should become ''her''."}'),
  ('ENG-0237', 'English', 'Direct and indirect speech', 'Commands and requests', 'moderate', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.14', 'Choose the correct indirect form of:
The doctor said to the patient, "Do not eat oily food."', '["The doctor advised the patient that not to eat oily food.", "The doctor told the patient that do not eat oily food.", "The doctor advised the patient do not eat oily food.", "The doctor advised the patient not to eat oily food."]', 3, 'A negative command is reported with a verb like ''advised'' or ''told'' + object + not to + verb.', '{"0": "''That'' cannot be followed by an infinitive here.", "1": "A command is not reported with ''that'' + imperative.", "2": "The infinitive ''to eat'' is needed after the object."}'),
  ('ENG-0238', 'English', 'Direct and indirect speech', 'Statements', 'easy', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.14', 'Choose the correct indirect form of:
He said, "I have finished my work."', '["He said that he had finished his work.", "He said that he has finished his work.", "He said that he finished my work.", "He said that I had finished my work."]', 0, 'Present perfect shifts back to past perfect, and ''I/my'' become ''he/his''.', '{"1": "The tense should shift back to ''had finished''.", "2": "''My'' should become ''his''.", "3": "The pronouns should change from ''I/my''."}'),
  ('ENG-0239', 'English', 'Direct and indirect speech', 'Questions', 'moderate', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.14', 'Choose the correct indirect form of:
She said to me, "Are you coming?"', '["She asked me was I coming.", "She asked me if I was coming.", "She asked me if you were coming.", "She said to me if I am coming."]', 1, 'A yes/no question is reported with ''if'' or ''whether'', statement word order, and a backshifted tense.', '{"0": "Question word order is not used in reported speech.", "2": "''You'' (the listener, me) should become ''I''.", "3": "''Said to'' becomes ''asked'', and ''am'' should shift to ''was''."}'),
  ('ENG-0240', 'English', 'Direct and indirect speech', 'Statements', 'hard', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.14', 'Choose the correct indirect form of:
They said, "We were playing cricket."', '["They said that they were been playing cricket.", "They said that we had been playing cricket.", "They said that they had been playing cricket.", "They said that they have been playing cricket."]', 2, 'Past continuous shifts back to past perfect continuous, and ''we'' becomes ''they''.', '{"0": "''Were been'' is not a verb form.", "1": "''We'' should become ''they''.", "3": "Present perfect continuous is not the backshifted form."}'),
  ('ENG-0241', 'English', 'Direct and indirect speech', 'Exclamations', 'moderate', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.14', 'Choose the correct indirect form of:
He said, "What a beautiful view!"', '["He said that what a beautiful view it was.", "He exclaimed what a beautiful view!", "He asked that it was a beautiful view.", "He exclaimed that it was a very beautiful view."]', 3, 'An exclamation is reported with ''exclaimed'' and turned into a statement, often with ''very''.', '{"0": "The exclamation form ''what a ...'' is not kept after ''that''.", "1": "The exclamation mark and form are not kept.", "2": "It is an exclamation, not a question."}'),
  ('ENG-0242', 'English', 'Direct and indirect speech', 'Indirect to direct', 'moderate', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.14', 'Choose the correct direct form of:
She told me that she had lost her pen.', '["She said to me, \"I have lost my pen.\"", "She said to me, \"She has lost her pen.\"", "She said to me, \"You have lost your pen.\"", "She said to me, \"I had lost her pen.\""]', 0, 'Going back to direct speech, ''she'' becomes ''I'', ''her'' becomes ''my'', and the past perfect returns to present perfect.', '{"1": "Inside quotation marks, she would say ''I'', not ''she''.", "2": "She lost her own pen, not the listener''s.", "3": "''Had lost'' and ''her'' are reported forms, not her actual words."}'),
  ('ENG-0243', 'English', 'Direct and indirect speech', 'Statements', 'easy', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.14', 'Choose the correct indirect form of:
He said, "I can swim."', '["He said that he can swam.", "He said that he could swim.", "He said that he could swam.", "He said that I could swim."]', 1, '''Can'' becomes ''could'', followed by the base verb.', '{"0": "''Can swam'' is ungrammatical.", "2": "A modal is followed by the base form ''swim''.", "3": "''I'' should become ''he''."}'),
  ('ENG-0244', 'English', 'Direct and indirect speech', 'Commands and requests', 'hard', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.14', 'Choose the correct indirect form of:
"Let''s go for a walk," she said.', '["She ordered them to go for a walk.", "She said that let us go for a walk.", "She suggested that they should go for a walk.", "She asked that they went for a walk?"]', 2, '''Let''s'' is a suggestion, reported with ''suggested that ... should'' (or ''suggested going'').', '{"0": "''Let''s'' is a suggestion, not an order.", "1": "''Let us'' is not kept after ''that''.", "3": "It is not a question."}'),
  ('ENG-0245', 'English', 'Direct and indirect speech', 'Statements', 'moderate', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.14', 'Choose the correct indirect form of:
He said, "I met her yesterday."', '["He said that he met her yesterday.", "He said that he has met her the day before.", "He said that I had met her the previous day.", "He said that he had met her the day before."]', 3, 'Simple past shifts to past perfect, and ''yesterday'' becomes ''the day before'' (the previous day).', '{"0": "The time word ''yesterday'' should change.", "1": "''Has met'' is present perfect.", "2": "''I'' should become ''he''."}'),
  ('ENG-0246', 'English', 'Direct and indirect speech', 'Statements', 'moderate', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 2.14', 'Choose the correct indirect form of:
She said, "This is my book."', '["She said that that was her book.", "She said that this is my book.", "She said that this was my book.", "She said that that is her book."]', 0, '''This'' becomes ''that'', ''is'' becomes ''was'', and ''my'' becomes ''her''.', '{"1": "Nothing has been changed.", "2": "''My'' should become ''her'' and ''this'' should become ''that''.", "3": "''Is'' should shift to ''was''."}'),
  ('ENG-0247', 'English', 'Subject-verb agreement', 'Neither...nor and either...or', 'moderate', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 3.3', 'Choose the option that correctly fills the blank:
Neither the students nor the teacher ______ present.', '["were", "was", "are", "have been"]', 1, 'With neither...nor, the verb agrees with the nearer subject, ''teacher'' (singular).', '{"0": "The nearer subject is singular.", "2": "The nearer subject is singular.", "3": "The nearer subject is singular."}'),
  ('ENG-0248', 'English', 'Subject-verb agreement', 'Words between subject and verb', 'moderate', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 3.3', 'Choose the option that correctly fills the blank:
The quality of these medicines ______ excellent.', '["are", "were", "is", "have been"]', 2, 'The subject is ''quality'' (singular); ''of these medicines'' is only a phrase describing it.', '{"0": "The verb agrees with ''quality'', not ''medicines''.", "1": "The verb agrees with ''quality'', not ''medicines''.", "3": "The verb agrees with ''quality'', not ''medicines''."}'),
  ('ENG-0249', 'English', 'Subject-verb agreement', 'Indefinite pronouns', 'easy', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 3.3', 'Choose the option that correctly fills the blank:
Each of the players ______ a medal.', '["receive", "are receiving", "have received", "receives"]', 3, '''Each'' is singular, so the verb is singular.', '{"0": "''Each'' needs a singular verb.", "1": "''Are'' is plural.", "2": "''Have'' is plural."}')
) AS s
JOIN users u ON u.email = 'hozaiphaa@gmail.com';

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions
  (id, subject, chapter, topic, difficulty, source, stem, options, answer, why, wrong, stage, free, checks, writer_id, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT s.column1, s.column2, s.column3, s.column4, s.column5, s.column6, s.column7, s.column8, s.column9, s.column10, s.column11,
  'live', 0, '[]', u.id, unixepoch() * 1000, unixepoch() * 1000
FROM (VALUES
  ('ENG-0250', 'English', 'Subject-verb agreement', 'Compound subjects', 'moderate', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 3.3', 'Choose the option that correctly fills the blank:
Bread and butter ______ my favourite breakfast.', '["is", "are", "were", "have been"]', 0, 'When two nouns joined by ''and'' name one thing or dish, the verb is singular.', '{"1": "''Bread and butter'' is one dish.", "2": "''Bread and butter'' is one dish.", "3": "''Bread and butter'' is one dish."}'),
  ('ENG-0251', 'English', 'Subject-verb agreement', 'Nouns ending in -s', 'easy', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 3.3', 'Choose the option that correctly fills the blank:
Mathematics ______ my favourite subject.', '["are", "is", "were", "have been"]', 1, 'Subject names ending in -s, like mathematics and physics, take a singular verb.', '{"0": "Mathematics is singular.", "2": "Mathematics is singular.", "3": "Mathematics is singular."}'),
  ('ENG-0252', 'English', 'Subject-verb agreement', 'A number of / the number of', 'moderate', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 3.3', 'Choose the option that correctly fills the blank:
A number of students ______ absent today.', '["is", "was", "are", "has been"]', 2, '''A number of'' means ''many'', so it takes a plural verb.', '{"0": "''A number of'' takes a plural verb.", "1": "''A number of'' takes a plural verb.", "3": "''A number of'' takes a plural verb."}'),
  ('ENG-0253', 'English', 'Subject-verb agreement', 'A number of / the number of', 'moderate', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 3.3', 'Choose the option that correctly fills the blank:
The number of dengue patients ______ increasing.', '["are", "were", "have been", "is"]', 3, '''The number of'' refers to one number, so it takes a singular verb.', '{"0": "''The number'' is singular.", "1": "''The number'' is singular.", "2": "''The number'' is singular."}'),
  ('ENG-0254', 'English', 'Subject-verb agreement', 'Amounts', 'hard', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 3.3', 'Choose the option that correctly fills the blank:
Ten kilometres ______ a long distance to walk.', '["is", "are", "were", "have been"]', 0, 'A distance, sum of money or period of time thought of as one amount takes a singular verb.', '{"1": "Ten kilometres is treated as one distance.", "2": "Ten kilometres is treated as one distance.", "3": "Ten kilometres is treated as one distance."}'),
  ('ENG-0255', 'English', 'Subject-verb agreement', 'Neither...nor and either...or', 'hard', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 3.3', 'Choose the option that correctly fills the blank:
Either my brothers or my father ______ going to the meeting.', '["are", "is", "were", "have been"]', 1, 'With either...or, the verb agrees with the nearer subject, ''father'' (singular).', '{"0": "The nearer subject is singular.", "2": "The nearer subject is singular.", "3": "The nearer subject is singular."}'),
  ('ENG-0256', 'English', 'Subject-verb agreement', 'Words between subject and verb', 'moderate', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 3.3', 'Choose the option that correctly fills the blank:
The teacher, along with her students, ______ visiting the museum.', '["are", "were", "is", "have been"]', 2, 'Phrases like ''along with'' and ''as well as'' do not change the subject; the subject is ''teacher''.', '{"0": "The subject is ''teacher'' (singular).", "1": "The subject is ''teacher'' (singular).", "3": "The subject is ''teacher'' (singular)."}'),
  ('ENG-0257', 'English', 'Subject-verb agreement', 'Words between subject and verb', 'easy', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 3.3', 'Choose the option that correctly fills the blank:
One of my friends ______ a doctor.', '["are", "were", "have been", "is"]', 3, 'The subject is ''one'', which is singular.', '{"0": "The subject is ''one'', not ''friends''.", "1": "The subject is ''one'', not ''friends''.", "2": "The subject is ''one'', not ''friends''."}'),
  ('ENG-0258', 'English', 'Subject-verb agreement', 'There is / there are', 'easy', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 3.3', 'Choose the option that correctly fills the blank:
There ______ many reasons for his failure.', '["are", "is", "was", "has been"]', 0, 'After ''there'', the verb agrees with the real subject that follows, ''reasons'' (plural).', '{"1": "The real subject ''reasons'' is plural.", "2": "The real subject ''reasons'' is plural.", "3": "The real subject ''reasons'' is plural."}'),
  ('ENG-0259', 'English', 'Subject-verb agreement', 'Nouns ending in -s', 'moderate', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 3.3', 'Choose the option that correctly fills the blank:
The scissors ______ on the table.', '["is", "are", "was", "has been"]', 1, 'Nouns for things with two parts, such as scissors and trousers, take a plural verb.', '{"0": "''Scissors'' takes a plural verb.", "2": "''Scissors'' takes a plural verb.", "3": "''Scissors'' takes a plural verb."}'),
  ('ENG-0260', 'English', 'Subject-verb agreement', 'Indefinite pronouns', 'easy', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 3.3', 'Choose the option that correctly fills the blank:
Everyone in the class ______ to pass the test.', '["want", "are wanting", "wants", "have wanted"]', 2, '''Everyone'' is singular, so the verb takes -s.', '{"0": "''Everyone'' needs a singular verb.", "1": "''Are'' is plural.", "3": "''Have'' is plural."}'),
  ('ENG-0261', 'English', 'Subject-verb agreement', 'Nouns ending in -s', 'easy', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 3.3', 'Choose the option that correctly fills the blank:
The news from the hospital ______ good.', '["are", "were", "have been", "is"]', 3, '''News'' looks plural but is an uncountable singular noun.', '{"0": "''News'' takes a singular verb.", "1": "''News'' takes a singular verb.", "2": "''News'' takes a singular verb."}'),
  ('ENG-0262', 'English', 'Subject-verb agreement', 'Neither...nor and either...or', 'hard', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 3.3', 'Choose the option that correctly fills the blank:
Not only the students but also the principal ______ happy with the result.', '["was", "were", "have been", "are"]', 0, 'With ''not only ... but also'', the verb agrees with the nearer subject, ''principal'' (singular).', '{"1": "The nearer subject is singular.", "2": "The nearer subject is singular.", "3": "The nearer subject is singular."}'),
  ('ENG-0263', 'English', 'Subject-verb agreement', 'Indefinite pronouns', 'hard', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 3.3', 'Choose the option that correctly fills the blank:
Many a student ______ failed this paper.', '["have", "has", "are", "were"]', 1, '''Many a'' is followed by a singular noun and a singular verb.', '{"0": "''Many a'' takes a singular verb.", "2": "''Many a'' takes a singular verb, and ''are failed'' is wrong here.", "3": "''Many a'' takes a singular verb."}'),
  ('ENG-0264', 'English', 'Subject-verb agreement', 'Compound subjects', 'easy', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 3.3', 'Choose the option that correctly fills the blank:
Physics and chemistry ______ both compulsory for MDCAT.', '["is", "was", "are", "has been"]', 2, 'Two separate subjects joined by ''and'' take a plural verb.', '{"0": "Two subjects joined by ''and'' are plural.", "1": "Two subjects joined by ''and'' are plural.", "3": "Two subjects joined by ''and'' are plural."}'),
  ('ENG-0265', 'English', 'Sentence correction and spelling', 'Spelling', 'moderate', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 3.4', 'Which word is spelled correctly?', '["accomodation", "acommodation", "accommodasion", "accommodation"]', 3, 'The correct spelling is ''accommodation''.', '{"0": "''accomodation'' is a misspelling of ''accommodation''.", "1": "''acommodation'' is a misspelling of ''accommodation''.", "2": "''accommodasion'' is a misspelling of ''accommodation''."}'),
  ('ENG-0266', 'English', 'Sentence correction and spelling', 'Spelling', 'easy', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 3.4', 'Which word is spelled correctly?', '["necessary", "neccessary", "necessery", "nessesary"]', 0, 'The correct spelling is ''necessary''.', '{"1": "''neccessary'' is a misspelling of ''necessary''.", "2": "''necessery'' is a misspelling of ''necessary''.", "3": "''nessesary'' is a misspelling of ''necessary''."}'),
  ('ENG-0267', 'English', 'Sentence correction and spelling', 'Spelling', 'hard', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 3.4', 'Which word is spelled correctly?', '["occurence", "occurrence", "ocurrence", "occurrance"]', 1, 'The correct spelling is ''occurrence''.', '{"0": "''occurence'' is a misspelling of ''occurrence''.", "2": "''ocurrence'' is a misspelling of ''occurrence''.", "3": "''occurrance'' is a misspelling of ''occurrence''."}'),
  ('ENG-0268', 'English', 'Sentence correction and spelling', 'Spelling', 'hard', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 3.4', 'Which word is spelled correctly?', '["embarass", "embarras", "embarrass", "emmbarrass"]', 2, 'The correct spelling is ''embarrass''.', '{"0": "''embarass'' is a misspelling of ''embarrass''.", "1": "''embarras'' is a misspelling of ''embarrass''.", "3": "''emmbarrass'' is a misspelling of ''embarrass''."}'),
  ('ENG-0269', 'English', 'Sentence correction and spelling', 'Spelling', 'moderate', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 3.4', 'Which word is spelled correctly?', '["seperate", "separete", "saperate", "separate"]', 3, 'The correct spelling is ''separate''.', '{"0": "''seperate'' is a misspelling of ''separate''.", "1": "''separete'' is a misspelling of ''separate''.", "2": "''saperate'' is a misspelling of ''separate''."}')
) AS s
JOIN users u ON u.email = 'hozaiphaa@gmail.com';

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions
  (id, subject, chapter, topic, difficulty, source, stem, options, answer, why, wrong, stage, free, checks, writer_id, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT s.column1, s.column2, s.column3, s.column4, s.column5, s.column6, s.column7, s.column8, s.column9, s.column10, s.column11,
  'live', 0, '[]', u.id, unixepoch() * 1000, unixepoch() * 1000
FROM (VALUES
  ('ENG-0270', 'English', 'Sentence correction and spelling', 'Spelling', 'easy', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 3.4', 'Which word is spelled correctly?', '["receive", "recieve", "receeve", "reiceve"]', 0, 'The correct spelling is ''receive''.', '{"1": "''recieve'' is a misspelling of ''receive''.", "2": "''receeve'' is a misspelling of ''receive''.", "3": "''reiceve'' is a misspelling of ''receive''."}'),
  ('ENG-0271', 'English', 'Sentence correction and spelling', 'Spelling', 'hard', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 3.4', 'Which word is spelled correctly?', '["priviledge", "privilege", "privilage", "previlege"]', 1, 'The correct spelling is ''privilege''.', '{"0": "''priviledge'' is a misspelling of ''privilege''.", "2": "''privilage'' is a misspelling of ''privilege''.", "3": "''previlege'' is a misspelling of ''privilege''."}'),
  ('ENG-0272', 'English', 'Sentence correction and spelling', 'Spelling', 'moderate', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 3.4', 'Which word is spelled correctly?', '["commitee", "comittee", "committee", "committe"]', 2, 'The correct spelling is ''committee''.', '{"0": "''commitee'' is a misspelling of ''committee''.", "1": "''comittee'' is a misspelling of ''committee''.", "3": "''committe'' is a misspelling of ''committee''."}'),
  ('ENG-0273', 'English', 'Sentence correction and spelling', 'Spelling', 'hard', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 3.4', 'Which word is spelled correctly?', '["diarhoea", "diarrhoae", "diarrohea", "diarrhoea"]', 3, 'The correct spelling is ''diarrhoea''.', '{"0": "''diarhoea'' is a misspelling of ''diarrhoea''.", "1": "''diarrhoae'' is a misspelling of ''diarrhoea''.", "2": "''diarrohea'' is a misspelling of ''diarrhoea''."}'),
  ('ENG-0274', 'English', 'Sentence correction and spelling', 'Choosing the correct sentence', 'easy', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 3.1', 'Which sentence is correct?', '["He is one of the best surgeons in the city.", "He is one of the best surgeon in the city.", "He is one of the better surgeons in the city.", "He is one of best surgeons in the city."]', 0, '''One of the'' is followed by a plural noun, and a superlative needs ''the''.', '{"1": "After ''one of the best'', the noun must be plural.", "2": "A comparison with a whole group needs the superlative ''best''.", "3": "The superlative needs ''the''."}'),
  ('ENG-0275', 'English', 'Sentence correction and spelling', 'Choosing the correct sentence', 'moderate', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 3.1', 'Which sentence is correct?', '["I have been waiting since two hours.", "I have been waiting for two hours.", "I am waiting since two hours.", "I am waiting for two hours."]', 1, 'A length of time takes ''for'', and an action continuing up to now takes the present perfect continuous.', '{"0": "''Since'' is for a starting point, not a duration.", "2": "Both the tense and the preposition are wrong.", "3": "The present continuous does not show duration up to now."}'),
  ('ENG-0276', 'English', 'Sentence correction and spelling', 'Parallel structure', 'moderate', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 3.1', 'Which sentence is correct?', '["She likes swimming, running and to cycle.", "She likes to swim, running and cycling.", "She likes swimming, running and cycling.", "She likes swimming, to run and cycling."]', 2, 'Items in a list should have the same grammatical form (parallel structure).', '{"0": "''To cycle'' breaks the pattern of -ing forms.", "1": "''To swim'' breaks the pattern.", "3": "''To run'' breaks the pattern."}'),
  ('ENG-0277', 'English', 'Sentence correction and spelling', 'Faulty comparison', 'hard', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 3.1', 'Which sentence is correct?', '["Lahore is bigger than any city in Punjab.", "Lahore is biggest than any other city in Punjab.", "Lahore is more bigger than any other city in Punjab.", "Lahore is bigger than any other city in Punjab."]', 3, 'When comparing something with the rest of its own group, use ''any other''; otherwise it is compared with itself.', '{"0": "Lahore is itself a city in Punjab, so it cannot be bigger than ''any city'' there.", "1": "''Biggest'' is superlative and cannot take ''than''.", "2": "''More bigger'' is a double comparative."}'),
  ('ENG-0278', 'English', 'Sentence correction and spelling', 'Double negatives', 'easy', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 3.1', 'Which sentence is correct?', '["I don''t know anything about it.", "I don''t know nothing about it.", "I don''t know nothing about nothing.", "I not know anything about it."]', 0, 'Standard English uses one negative per clause: don''t ... anything.', '{"1": "This is a double negative.", "2": "This uses several negatives.", "3": "A negative with a main verb needs ''do not''."}'),
  ('ENG-0279', 'English', 'Sentence correction and spelling', 'Faulty comparison', 'easy', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 3.1', 'Which sentence is correct?', '["She is more taller than her sister.", "She is taller than her sister.", "She is tallest than her sister.", "She is most tall than her sister."]', 1, 'One-syllable adjectives form the comparative with -er alone.', '{"0": "''More taller'' is a double comparative.", "2": "A superlative cannot take ''than''.", "3": "''Most'' is superlative and does not go with ''than''."}'),
  ('ENG-0280', 'English', 'Sentence correction and spelling', 'Articles', 'easy', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 3.1', 'Which sentence is correct?', '["He is a honest man.", "He is honest a man.", "He is an honest man.", "He is the honest man."]', 2, '''Honest'' begins with a vowel sound (the h is silent), so it takes ''an''.', '{"0": "''A'' is used before consonant sounds; ''honest'' starts with a vowel sound.", "1": "The article must come before the adjective.", "3": "''The'' changes the meaning to a particular known man."}'),
  ('ENG-0281', 'English', 'Sentence correction and spelling', 'Articles', 'hard', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 3.1', 'Which sentence is correct?', '["She is a MBBS student.", "She is MBBS student.", "She is the MBBS student of.", "She is an MBBS student."]', 3, 'The choice of article depends on sound: ''MBBS'' begins with the vowel sound ''em'', so it takes ''an''.', '{"0": "''M'' is pronounced ''em'', a vowel sound.", "1": "A singular countable noun needs an article.", "2": "This sentence is incomplete."}'),
  ('ENG-0282', 'English', 'Sentence correction and spelling', 'Unnecessary prepositions', 'moderate', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 3.1', 'Which sentence is correct?', '["Please discuss the problem with your supervisor.", "Please discuss about the problem with your supervisor.", "Please discuss on the problem with your supervisor.", "Please discuss over the problem with your supervisor."]', 0, '''Discuss'' is a transitive verb and takes a direct object without a preposition.', '{"1": "''Discuss about'' is a common error.", "2": "''Discuss on'' is a common error.", "3": "''Discuss over'' is a common error."}'),
  ('ENG-0283', 'English', 'Sentence correction and spelling', 'Faulty comparison', 'moderate', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 3.1', 'Which sentence is correct?', '["He is senior than me.", "He is senior to me.", "He is more senior than me.", "He is senior from me."]', 1, 'Latin comparatives such as senior, junior, superior and inferior take ''to'', not ''than''.', '{"0": "''Senior'' takes ''to'', not ''than''.", "2": "''Senior'' is already comparative.", "3": "''Senior from'' is incorrect."}'),
  ('ENG-0284', 'English', 'Sentence correction and spelling', 'Unnecessary prepositions', 'moderate', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 3.1', 'Which sentence is correct?', '["I prefer reading than watching television.", "I prefer reading rather watching television.", "I prefer reading to watching television.", "I prefer reading from watching television."]', 2, '''Prefer A to B'' is the correct pattern.', '{"0": "''Prefer ... than'' is a common error.", "1": "''Rather'' would need ''than'' and a different structure.", "3": "''From'' is not used with prefer."}'),
  ('ENG-0285', 'English', 'Sentence correction and spelling', 'Unnecessary prepositions', 'moderate', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 3.1', 'Which sentence is correct?', '["Despite of the rain, the match continued.", "In spite the rain, the match continued.", "Despite of rain, the match continued.", "Despite the rain, the match continued."]', 3, '''Despite'' is used without ''of''; ''in spite'' needs ''of''.', '{"0": "''Despite of'' is incorrect.", "1": "''In spite'' must be followed by ''of''.", "2": "''Despite of'' is incorrect."}'),
  ('ENG-0286', 'English', 'Sentence correction and spelling', 'Spotting the error', 'moderate', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 3.1', 'Which part of the sentence contains an error?
(A) Each of the boys (B) have brought (C) his own (D) lunch box.', '["(B) have brought", "(A) Each of the boys", "(C) his own", "(D) lunch box."]', 0, '''Each'' is singular, so the verb should be ''has brought''.', '{"1": "''Each of the boys'' is a correct subject.", "2": "''His own'' correctly matches the singular ''each''.", "3": "''Lunch box'' is correct."}'),
  ('ENG-0287', 'English', 'Sentence correction and spelling', 'Spotting the error', 'moderate', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 3.1', 'Which part of the sentence contains an error?
(A) She has been (B) working here (C) since five years (D) and enjoys it.', '["(A) She has been", "(C) since five years", "(B) working here", "(D) and enjoys it."]', 1, 'A length of time takes ''for'': ''for five years''.', '{"0": "''Has been'' is correct for an action continuing to now.", "2": "''Working here'' is correct.", "3": "''And enjoys it'' is correct."}'),
  ('ENG-0288', 'English', 'Sentence correction and spelling', 'Spotting the error', 'hard', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 3.1', 'Which part of the sentence contains an error?
(A) If I was you, (B) I would accept (C) the offer (D) without delay.', '["(B) I would accept", "(C) the offer", "(A) If I was you,", "(D) without delay."]', 2, 'In an unreal condition, formal English uses ''were'' for all persons: ''If I were you''.', '{"0": "''Would accept'' correctly follows an unreal if-clause.", "1": "''The offer'' is correct.", "3": "''Without delay'' is correct."}'),
  ('ENG-0289', 'English', 'Sentence correction and spelling', 'Spotting the error', 'hard', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 3.1', 'Which part of the sentence contains an error?
(A) The reason he failed (B) is because (C) he did not (D) revise.', '["(A) The reason he failed", "(C) he did not", "(D) revise.", "(B) is because"]', 3, '''The reason ... is because'' repeats the idea of cause; the correct form is ''the reason ... is that''.', '{"0": "''The reason he failed'' is a correct subject.", "1": "''He did not'' is correct.", "2": "''Revise'' is correct after ''did not''."}')
) AS s
JOIN users u ON u.email = 'hozaiphaa@gmail.com';

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions
  (id, subject, chapter, topic, difficulty, source, stem, options, answer, why, wrong, stage, free, checks, writer_id, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT s.column1, s.column2, s.column3, s.column4, s.column5, s.column6, s.column7, s.column8, s.column9, s.column10, s.column11,
  'live', 0, '[]', u.id, unixepoch() * 1000, unixepoch() * 1000
FROM (VALUES
  ('ENG-0290', 'English', 'Sentence correction and spelling', 'Spotting the error', 'moderate', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 3.1', 'Which part of the sentence contains an error?
(A) Neither of the (B) two answers (C) are (D) correct.', '["(C) are", "(A) Neither of the", "(B) two answers", "(D) correct."]', 0, '''Neither'' is singular, so the verb should be ''is''.', '{"1": "''Neither of the'' is correct.", "2": "''Two answers'' is correct.", "3": "''Correct'' is correct."}'),
  ('ENG-0291', 'English', 'Sentence correction and spelling', 'Spotting the error', 'moderate', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 3.1', 'Which part of the sentence contains an error?
(A) The doctor (B) advised him (C) to avoid (D) to eat sweets.', '["(A) The doctor", "(D) to eat sweets.", "(B) advised him", "(C) to avoid"]', 1, '''Avoid'' is followed by a gerund, so it should be ''avoid eating sweets''.', '{"0": "''The doctor'' is correct.", "2": "''Advised him'' is correct.", "3": "''To avoid'' correctly follows ''advised him''."}'),
  ('ENG-0292', 'English', 'Sentence correction and spelling', 'Articles', 'easy', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 3.4', 'Choose the option that correctly fills the blank:
She wants to become ______ orthopaedic surgeon.', '["a", "the", "an", "no article"]', 2, '''Orthopaedic'' begins with a vowel sound, so it takes ''an''; the surgeon is not a particular known one, so not ''the''.', '{"0": "''A'' is used before consonant sounds.", "1": "''The'' would point to one particular, known surgeon.", "3": "A singular countable noun needs an article here."}'),
  ('ENG-0293', 'English', 'Sentence correction and spelling', 'Articles', 'moderate', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 3.4', 'Choose the option that correctly fills the blank:
______ Indus is the longest river in Pakistan.', '["A", "An", "No article", "The"]', 3, 'Names of rivers take the definite article: the Indus.', '{"0": "''A'' cannot be used before a unique named river.", "1": "''An'' cannot be used before a unique named river.", "2": "River names need ''the''."}'),
  ('ENG-0294', 'English', 'Sentence correction and spelling', 'Articles', 'hard', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 3.4', 'Choose the option that correctly fills the blank:
There is ______ university near our house.', '["a", "an", "the", "no article"]', 0, '''University'' begins with a ''yoo'' (consonant) sound, so it takes ''a''.', '{"1": "The letter u here sounds like ''yoo'', a consonant sound.", "2": "''There is'' introduces something new, so the indefinite article is used.", "3": "A singular countable noun needs an article here."}'),
  ('ENG-0295', 'English', 'Sentence correction and spelling', 'Word order', 'moderate', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, English learning outcome 3.2', 'Which sentence has the correct word order?', '["Can you tell me where is the pharmacy?", "Can you tell me where the pharmacy is?", "Can you tell me the pharmacy where is?", "Where you can tell me the pharmacy is?"]', 1, 'In an embedded (indirect) question, the subject comes before the verb: where the pharmacy is.', '{"0": "Question order (is the pharmacy) is not used inside an embedded question.", "2": "The words are out of order.", "3": "The main question needs ''Can you'' at the start."}')
) AS s
JOIN users u ON u.email = 'hozaiphaa@gmail.com';

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions
  (id, subject, chapter, topic, difficulty, source, stem, options, answer, why, wrong, stage, free, checks, writer_id, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT s.column1, s.column2, s.column3, s.column4, s.column5, s.column6, s.column7, s.column8, s.column9, s.column10, s.column11,
  'live', 0, '[]', u.id, unixepoch() * 1000, unixepoch() * 1000
FROM (VALUES
  ('LR-0010', 'Logical Reasoning', 'Critical thinking', 'Assumptions', 'moderate', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, Logical Reasoning learning outcome 5.1', '"Ali must be a good doctor because he graduated from a famous medical college."
Which assumption does this argument depend on?', '["Ali studied harder than his classmates", "Famous colleges charge high fees", "Graduates of famous medical colleges are good doctors", "Good doctors always work in famous hospitals"]', 2, 'The conclusion (Ali is a good doctor) follows only if graduating from a famous college makes someone a good doctor. That unstated link is the assumption.', '{"0": "The argument never mentions how hard Ali studied.", "1": "Fees have nothing to do with the conclusion.", "3": "Where good doctors work is not part of the argument."}'),
  ('LR-0011', 'Logical Reasoning', 'Critical thinking', 'Weakening an argument', 'hard', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, Logical Reasoning learning outcome 5.1', '"Students who eat breakfast score higher in tests, so eating breakfast improves test scores."
Which fact, if true, most WEAKENS this conclusion?', '["Breakfast is the first meal of the day", "Some students prefer tea to milk at breakfast", "Test scores are given out of 100", "Students who eat breakfast also tend to sleep longer and study more"]', 3, 'If breakfast-eaters also sleep and study more, those factors could explain the higher scores, so breakfast may not be the cause.', '{"0": "This is a definition and does not affect the argument.", "1": "Drink preference is irrelevant to the conclusion.", "2": "How scores are marked does not challenge the causal claim."}'),
  ('LR-0012', 'Logical Reasoning', 'Critical thinking', 'Logical fallacies', 'moderate', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, Logical Reasoning learning outcome 5.1', '"You cannot trust Dr. Kamal''s research on smoking; he is a rude man."
What is wrong with this reasoning?', '["It attacks the person instead of the evidence", "It generalises from too few examples", "It offers only two choices when more exist", "It assumes that what is popular must be true"]', 0, 'This is an ad hominem fallacy: Dr. Kamal''s manners say nothing about whether his research is sound.', '{"1": "That would be a hasty generalisation; no examples are counted here.", "2": "That would be a false dilemma; no choices are offered here.", "3": "That would be an appeal to popularity; popularity is not mentioned."}'),
  ('LR-0013', 'Logical Reasoning', 'Critical thinking', 'Logical fallacies', 'easy', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, Logical Reasoning learning outcome 5.1', '"I met two rude shopkeepers in one market, so all shopkeepers in this city are rude."
This reasoning is an example of:', '["a valid deduction", "a hasty generalisation", "an appeal to authority", "circular reasoning"]', 1, 'A conclusion about all shopkeepers is drawn from only two cases, which is far too small a sample.', '{"0": "The conclusion does not follow necessarily from the premises.", "2": "No expert or authority is quoted.", "3": "The conclusion is not simply restated as the reason."}'),
  ('LR-0014', 'Logical Reasoning', 'Critical thinking', 'Logical fallacies', 'moderate', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, Logical Reasoning learning outcome 5.1', '"Either you support every part of this new timetable, or you want the college to fail."
This reasoning is flawed because it:', '["attacks the speaker''s character", "relies on a famous person''s opinion", "presents only two options when others exist", "repeats the conclusion as its reason"]', 2, 'This is a false dilemma: one could support parts of the timetable, or oppose it while still wanting the college to succeed.', '{"0": "No one''s character is attacked.", "1": "No famous person is quoted.", "3": "The conclusion is not used as its own reason."}'),
  ('LR-0015', 'Logical Reasoning', 'Critical thinking', 'Logical fallacies', 'easy', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, Logical Reasoning learning outcome 5.1', '"Millions of people use this herbal cure, so it must work."
What is the flaw in this reasoning?', '["Herbs can never be medicines", "Millions is an exaggeration", "It attacks the people who use the cure", "Popularity does not prove effectiveness"]', 3, 'How many people use something is not evidence that it works; only proper testing can show that.', '{"0": "Some medicines do come from plants, so this is false.", "1": "The number is not the flaw; the reasoning is.", "2": "No one is attacked."}'),
  ('LR-0016', 'Logical Reasoning', 'Critical thinking', 'Logical fallacies', 'moderate', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, Logical Reasoning learning outcome 5.1', '"This book is reliable because the book itself says that everything in it is true."
This is an example of:', '["circular reasoning", "a hasty generalisation", "a false dilemma", "a valid argument"]', 0, 'The argument uses the claim it is trying to prove (the book is reliable) as its own evidence.', '{"1": "No small sample is generalised.", "2": "No choice between two options is forced.", "3": "A claim cannot prove itself, so it is not valid support."}'),
  ('LR-0017', 'Logical Reasoning', 'Critical thinking', 'Logical fallacies', 'hard', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, Logical Reasoning learning outcome 5.1', 'A: "We should reduce the sugar in canteen food."
B: "So you want to ban every tasty food in the college?"
B''s reply is an example of:', '["an appeal to popularity", "a straw man", "a slippery slope", "a hasty generalisation"]', 1, 'B twists A''s moderate proposal into an extreme one that is easier to attack. That is a straw man.', '{"0": "B does not claim something is true because many people believe it.", "2": "B does not describe a chain of steps leading to disaster.", "3": "B does not generalise from a few examples."}'),
  ('LR-0018', 'Logical Reasoning', 'Critical thinking', 'Logical fallacies', 'moderate', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, Logical Reasoning learning outcome 5.1', '"A famous cricketer says this toothpaste prevents tooth decay, so it must be true."
What is the flaw?', '["Toothpaste can never prevent decay", "The argument attacks dentists", "A cricketer is not an expert on dental health", "The claim is about cricket, not teeth"]', 2, 'This is an appeal to an unqualified authority: fame in sport gives no expertise in dentistry.', '{"0": "Some toothpastes do help prevent decay; that is not the flaw.", "1": "Dentists are not attacked.", "3": "The claim is about teeth; the problem is who is making it."}'),
  ('LR-0019', 'Logical Reasoning', 'Critical thinking', 'Logical fallacies', 'moderate', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, Logical Reasoning learning outcome 5.1', '"If we allow calculators in class, students will forget how to add, then they will fail every exam, and soon the whole country will be unable to count."
This reasoning is an example of:', '["a straw man", "circular reasoning", "a valid argument", "a slippery slope"]', 3, 'A slippery slope claims that one small step will lead, without evidence, to a chain of extreme results.', '{"0": "No opponent''s view is distorted.", "1": "The conclusion is not used as its own reason.", "2": "Each step is assumed, not shown, so the argument is not valid."}'),
  ('LR-0020', 'Logical Reasoning', 'Critical thinking', 'Fact and opinion', 'easy', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, Logical Reasoning learning outcome 5.1', 'Which of these is a FACT rather than an opinion?', '["At sea level, pure water boils at 100 °C.", "Biology is the most interesting subject.", "Summer is the best season.", "Karachi has the tastiest food in Pakistan."]', 0, 'A fact can be checked and proved; the boiling point of water can be measured.', '{"1": "''Most interesting'' is a personal judgement.", "2": "''Best season'' is a matter of taste.", "3": "''Tastiest'' is a matter of taste."}'),
  ('LR-0021', 'Logical Reasoning', 'Critical thinking', 'Arguments and conclusions', 'easy', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, Logical Reasoning learning outcome 5.1', '"The roads are wet and people are carrying umbrellas, so it must have rained recently."
What is the conclusion of this argument?', '["The roads are wet", "It must have rained recently", "People are carrying umbrellas", "Umbrellas keep people dry"]', 1, 'The conclusion is the claim the evidence is meant to support; it follows ''so''.', '{"0": "This is evidence (a premise).", "2": "This is evidence (a premise).", "3": "This is not stated in the argument."}'),
  ('LR-0022', 'Logical Reasoning', 'Critical thinking', 'Weakening an argument', 'hard', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, Logical Reasoning learning outcome 5.1', '"Cities with more hospitals record more deaths each year, so hospitals cause deaths."
What is the main flaw in this argument?', '["Hospitals are expensive to build", "Some cities have no hospitals", "Bigger cities have both more people and more hospitals, and seriously ill people go to hospitals", "Death records are kept by hospitals"]', 2, 'A link between two things does not prove one causes the other. Population size and the fact that the sick go to hospitals explain the pattern.', '{"0": "Cost is irrelevant to the causal claim.", "1": "This does not explain the link the argument relies on.", "3": "Who keeps the records does not address the flawed reasoning."}'),
  ('LR-0023', 'Logical Reasoning', 'Critical thinking', 'Evaluating evidence', 'moderate', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, Logical Reasoning learning outcome 5.1', 'A survey of 20 students at one academy found that 18 wanted longer holidays. A newspaper headline said: "Pakistan''s students want longer holidays."
What is the main problem with the headline?', '["18 out of 20 is less than half", "Students never want longer holidays", "Surveys can only be done by the government", "The sample is too small and not representative"]', 3, 'Twenty students from a single academy cannot stand for all the students of a country.', '{"0": "18 out of 20 is 90%, far more than half.", "1": "This is an unsupported claim, not the flaw.", "2": "Anyone can run a survey; the problem is the sample."}'),
  ('LR-0024', 'Logical Reasoning', 'Critical thinking', 'Necessary and sufficient conditions', 'hard', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, Logical Reasoning learning outcome 5.1', '"Passing MDCAT is necessary for admission to a public medical college in Pakistan."
If this is true, which statement MUST also be true?', '["Every student admitted to a public medical college has passed MDCAT", "Every student who passes MDCAT is admitted to a public medical college", "Passing MDCAT is enough to get admission", "Some students are admitted to public medical colleges without passing MDCAT"]', 0, 'A necessary condition must be met by everyone who gets the result. So every admitted student passed MDCAT.', '{"1": "This treats a necessary condition as a sufficient one.", "2": "''Enough'' means sufficient, which the statement does not say.", "3": "This contradicts the statement."}'),
  ('LR-0025', 'Logical Reasoning', 'Critical thinking', 'Weakening an argument', 'moderate', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, Logical Reasoning learning outcome 5.1', '"Vitamin X cures the common cold, because people who take it recover within a week."
Which fact, if true, most weakens this claim?', '["Vitamin X is sold in many pharmacies", "Most colds go away within a week without any treatment", "Vitamin X tablets are orange", "Some people catch colds in winter"]', 1, 'If colds clear up in a week anyway, recovering within a week shows nothing about Vitamin X.', '{"0": "Availability says nothing about effectiveness.", "2": "Colour is irrelevant.", "3": "When people catch colds does not test the claim."}'),
  ('LR-0026', 'Logical Reasoning', 'Critical thinking', 'Strengthening an argument', 'hard', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, Logical Reasoning learning outcome 5.1', '"The new cycle lanes have reduced traffic jams in our town."
Which fact, if true, most STRENGTHENS this claim?', '["Cycling is good exercise", "The lanes are painted green", "Car journeys fell after the lanes opened, while nearby towns without lanes saw no change", "Some people do not own bicycles"]', 2, 'A drop in car use in this town, with no drop in similar towns, links the change to the cycle lanes.', '{"0": "Health benefits do not show an effect on traffic.", "1": "Colour is irrelevant.", "3": "This, if anything, slightly weakens the claim."}'),
  ('LR-0027', 'Logical Reasoning', 'Critical thinking', 'True and false beliefs', 'moderate', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, Logical Reasoning learning outcome 5.1.3', 'Which belief can be shown to be FALSE using logical reasoning and evidence?', '["\"Washing hands reduces the spread of germs.\"", "\"Vaccines help the body build immunity.\"", "\"Smoking increases the risk of lung cancer.\"", "\"Antibiotics cure viral infections such as the common cold.\""]', 3, 'Antibiotics act on bacteria, not viruses, so they do not cure colds, which are viral.', '{"0": "This is well supported by evidence.", "1": "This is well supported by evidence.", "2": "This is well supported by evidence."}'),
  ('LR-0028', 'Logical Reasoning', 'Patterns and sequences', 'Number series', 'easy', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, Logical Reasoning learning outcome 5.2.2', 'What comes next in the series?
2, 6, 18, 54, ?', '["162", "108", "72", "216"]', 0, 'Each term is 3 times the one before: 54 × 3 = 162.', '{"1": "108 is 54 × 2.", "2": "72 adds 18; the rule is multiply by 3.", "3": "216 is 54 × 4."}'),
  ('LR-0029', 'Logical Reasoning', 'Patterns and sequences', 'Number series', 'moderate', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, Logical Reasoning learning outcome 5.2.1', 'What comes next in the series?
3, 7, 15, 31, 63, ?', '["126", "127", "95", "125"]', 1, 'Each term is double the previous plus 1: 63 × 2 + 1 = 127.', '{"0": "126 is 63 × 2 without adding 1.", "2": "95 adds 32; the gaps double each time.", "3": "125 is 63 × 2 − 1."}')
) AS s
JOIN users u ON u.email = 'hozaiphaa@gmail.com';

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions
  (id, subject, chapter, topic, difficulty, source, stem, options, answer, why, wrong, stage, free, checks, writer_id, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT s.column1, s.column2, s.column3, s.column4, s.column5, s.column6, s.column7, s.column8, s.column9, s.column10, s.column11,
  'live', 0, '[]', u.id, unixepoch() * 1000, unixepoch() * 1000
FROM (VALUES
  ('LR-0030', 'Logical Reasoning', 'Patterns and sequences', 'Number series', 'easy', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, Logical Reasoning learning outcome 5.2.1', 'What comes next in the series?
1, 4, 9, 16, 25, ?', '["30", "35", "36", "49"]', 2, 'The terms are squares: 1², 2², 3², 4², 5², so next is 6² = 36.', '{"0": "30 adds 5; the gaps increase by 2.", "1": "35 does not fit the squares.", "3": "49 is 7², skipping 6²."}'),
  ('LR-0031', 'Logical Reasoning', 'Patterns and sequences', 'Number series', 'moderate', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, Logical Reasoning learning outcome 5.2.1', 'What comes next in the series?
7, 10, 16, 25, 37, ?', '["49", "50", "55", "52"]', 3, 'The differences are 3, 6, 9, 12, so the next difference is 15: 37 + 15 = 52.', '{"0": "49 adds 12 again.", "1": "50 adds 13.", "2": "55 adds 18."}'),
  ('LR-0032', 'Logical Reasoning', 'Patterns and sequences', 'Number series', 'easy', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, Logical Reasoning learning outcome 5.2.1', 'What comes next in the series?
2, 3, 5, 7, 11, 13, ?', '["17", "15", "19", "16"]', 0, 'These are prime numbers in order; the prime after 13 is 17.', '{"1": "15 = 3 × 5 is not prime.", "2": "19 is prime but skips 17.", "3": "16 is not prime."}'),
  ('LR-0033', 'Logical Reasoning', 'Patterns and sequences', 'Number series', 'moderate', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, Logical Reasoning learning outcome 5.2.1', 'What comes next in the series?
1, 1, 2, 3, 5, 8, ?', '["11", "13", "12", "16"]', 1, 'Each term is the sum of the two before it: 5 + 8 = 13.', '{"0": "11 adds 3.", "2": "12 adds 4.", "3": "16 doubles 8."}'),
  ('LR-0034', 'Logical Reasoning', 'Patterns and sequences', 'Number series', 'moderate', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, Logical Reasoning learning outcome 5.2.1', 'What comes next in the series?
120, 60, 20, 5, ?', '["0", "2.5", "1", "4"]', 2, 'Divide by 2, then 3, then 4, then 5: 5 ÷ 5 = 1.', '{"0": "Nothing in the rule gives 0.", "1": "2.5 divides by 2 again.", "3": "4 subtracts 1."}'),
  ('LR-0035', 'Logical Reasoning', 'Patterns and sequences', 'Number series', 'easy', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, Logical Reasoning learning outcome 5.2.1', 'What comes next in the series?
1, 8, 27, 64, ?', '["100", "81", "128", "125"]', 3, 'The terms are cubes: 1³, 2³, 3³, 4³, so next is 5³ = 125.', '{"0": "100 is 10², not a cube in this pattern.", "1": "81 is 3⁴.", "2": "128 is 64 × 2."}'),
  ('LR-0036', 'Logical Reasoning', 'Patterns and sequences', 'Number series', 'easy', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, Logical Reasoning learning outcome 5.2.2', 'What comes next in the series?
81, 27, 9, 3, ?', '["1", "0", "−3", "1/9"]', 0, 'Each term is one third of the one before: 3 ÷ 3 = 1.', '{"1": "Dividing by 3 never gives 0.", "2": "Subtracting gives a wrong rule.", "3": "1/9 comes two terms later (1, 1/3, 1/9)."}'),
  ('LR-0037', 'Logical Reasoning', 'Patterns and sequences', 'Number series', 'moderate', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, Logical Reasoning learning outcome 5.2.1', 'What comes next in the series?
3, 5, 9, 17, 33, ?', '["66", "65", "49", "64"]', 1, 'Each term is double the previous minus 1: 33 × 2 − 1 = 65. (The gaps 2, 4, 8, 16 also double: 33 + 32 = 65.)', '{"0": "66 is 33 × 2 without subtracting 1.", "2": "49 adds 16 again.", "3": "64 is 2⁶ but does not fit the rule."}'),
  ('LR-0038', 'Logical Reasoning', 'Patterns and sequences', 'Number series', 'hard', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, Logical Reasoning learning outcome 5.2.2', 'What comes next in the series?
2, 4, 12, 48, 240, ?', '["480", "960", "1440", "1200"]', 2, 'Multiply by 2, 3, 4, 5, then 6: 240 × 6 = 1440.', '{"0": "480 multiplies by 2.", "1": "960 multiplies by 4.", "3": "1200 multiplies by 5 again."}'),
  ('LR-0039', 'Logical Reasoning', 'Patterns and sequences', 'Number series', 'moderate', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, Logical Reasoning learning outcome 5.2.1', 'Find the missing number:
6, 11, ?, 27, 38', '["16", "17", "20", "18"]', 3, 'The differences are 5, 7, 9, 11: 6 + 5 = 11, 11 + 7 = 18, 18 + 9 = 27, 27 + 11 = 38.', '{"0": "16 gives differences 5, 5, 11, 11.", "1": "17 gives differences 5, 6, 10, 11.", "2": "20 gives differences 5, 9, 7, 11."}'),
  ('LR-0040', 'Logical Reasoning', 'Patterns and sequences', 'Odd one out', 'hard', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, Logical Reasoning learning outcome 5.2.1', 'Which number does NOT fit the series?
2, 5, 10, 17, 26, 37, 50, 64', '["64", "17", "37", "50"]', 0, 'The terms follow n² + 1: 2, 5, 10, 17, 26, 37, 50, 65. The last term should be 8² + 1 = 65, so 64 is wrong.', '{"1": "17 = 4² + 1 fits.", "2": "37 = 6² + 1 fits.", "3": "50 = 7² + 1 fits."}'),
  ('LR-0041', 'Logical Reasoning', 'Patterns and sequences', 'Number series', 'moderate', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, Logical Reasoning learning outcome 5.2.1', 'What comes next in the series?
1, 10, 3, 20, 5, 30, ?', '["40", "7", "6", "35"]', 1, 'Two series alternate: 1, 3, 5, ... and 10, 20, 30, .... The next term belongs to the first series: 7.', '{"0": "40 is the term after 7, from the second series.", "2": "6 breaks the +2 pattern.", "3": "35 does not fit either series."}'),
  ('LR-0042', 'Logical Reasoning', 'Patterns and sequences', 'Number series', 'moderate', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, Logical Reasoning learning outcome 5.2.2', 'What comes next in the series?
2, −6, 18, −54, ?', '["−162", "108", "162", "−108"]', 2, 'Each term is multiplied by −3: −54 × −3 = +162.', '{"0": "The sign alternates, so after a negative term comes a positive one.", "1": "108 is 54 × 2.", "3": "−108 has the wrong sign and factor."}'),
  ('LR-0043', 'Logical Reasoning', 'Patterns and sequences', 'Number series', 'moderate', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, Logical Reasoning learning outcome 5.2.1', 'What comes next in the series?
0, 2, 6, 12, 20, 30, ?', '["40", "36", "44", "42"]', 3, 'The differences are 2, 4, 6, 8, 10, so the next is 12: 30 + 12 = 42. (Each term is n(n + 1).)', '{"0": "40 adds 10 again.", "1": "36 adds 6.", "2": "44 adds 14."}'),
  ('LR-0044', 'Logical Reasoning', 'Patterns and sequences', 'Number series', 'moderate', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, Logical Reasoning learning outcome 5.2.2', 'What comes next in the series?
1, 2, 6, 24, 120, ?', '["720", "240", "600", "840"]', 0, 'Multiply by 2, 3, 4, 5, then 6: 120 × 6 = 720.', '{"1": "240 multiplies by 2.", "2": "600 multiplies by 5 again.", "3": "840 multiplies by 7."}'),
  ('LR-0045', 'Logical Reasoning', 'Patterns and sequences', 'Letter series', 'easy', 'Examwise original question. PMDC MDCAT curriculum 2025, Logical Reasoning learning outcome 5.2.3', 'What comes next in the series?
